"""Authored mathematics topic depth: concrete, applied and diagnostic examples."""
from . import P, E

P('0-propositions-statements', 'Propositional logic', r'''
Think of a compound proposition as a small circuit whose inputs are truth values and whose gates are connectives. Translating a sentence therefore requires identifying its grouping, not just replacing every “and” or “or” with a symbol. A valid argument preserves truth from premises to conclusion under every assignment; it need not have premises that actually hold in the world. To analyze an argument, search specifically for an assignment making all premises true and the conclusion false. If none exists, the argument is valid. This method assumes classical two-valued logic and inclusive OR. A true conclusion on one assignment cannot repair an invalid argument structure.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Let P be true and Q false. Evaluate (P∨Q)∧¬Q.', r'Inner disjunction::T\lor F=T::Inclusive OR needs only one true input.~Negate Q::\neg F=T::Negation reverses the false value of Q.~Combine::T\land T=T::Both conjuncts are true, so the whole proposition is true.', 'True.', 'Rewriting the expression as (P∧¬Q)∨(Q∧¬Q) gives true OR false, again true.'),
E('application', 'A system satisfies P→Q and P. Must Q hold?', r'Assume a failing conclusion::Q=F::Validity is tested by trying to falsify the conclusion while retaining the premises.~Use the second premise::P=T::The premise P rules out every assignment with a false antecedent.~Test the first premise::T\to F=F::The remaining candidate violates P→Q, so no counterexample satisfies both premises.', 'Yes; the inference is valid modus ponens.', 'The only assignment satisfying both premises has P=Q=T; exhaustive two-variable checking agrees.'),
E('trap', 'Is the inference from P→Q and Q to P valid?', r'Try a false conclusion::P=F::An invalidity witness must make the proposed conclusion false.~Make the second premise true::Q=T::This assignment satisfies the premise Q.~Check the implication::F\to T=T::Both premises are true while P is false, furnishing a counterexample.', 'Invalid: affirming the consequent.', 'For example, rain may imply a wet road, but a sprinkler can make the road wet without rain.'))

P('0-propositions-statements', 'Truth tables', r'''
A truth table is exhaustive enumeration, so its main discipline is keeping assignments and intermediate columns consistent. With n distinct variables there are exactly 2 to the n rows regardless of how often a variable occurs in the expression. Repeated occurrences must share one truth value on a row. Number the rows systematically, compute negations and parenthesized parts first, and use the final column to count satisfying assignments or classify the formula. When an expression has several clauses, a short case split can independently check that count. This assumes all variable assignments are allowed; restricting the inputs by additional premises changes which rows are relevant to an argument.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Build the final column of (P∨Q)∧¬(P∧Q), in row order TT,TF,FT,FF.', r'Compute the disjunction::P\lor Q:\quad T,T,T,F::At least one true input makes OR true.~Compute the negated conjunction::\neg(P\land Q):\quad F,T,T,T::Only the first row has a true conjunction.~Combine columns::(P\lor Q)\land\neg(P\land Q):\quad F,T,T,F::AND keeps only rows where both intermediate columns are true.', 'The column is F,T,T,F: exactly one input must be true.', 'Directly counting assignments with unequal truth values gives precisely TF and FT.'),
E('application', 'How many of the eight assignments satisfy (P∨Q)∧(¬P∨R)?', r'Split on false P::P=F\Rightarrow Q\land T=Q::Q must be true, while R is free, giving two rows.~Split on true P::P=T\Rightarrow T\land R=R::R must be true, while Q is free, giving two more rows.~Add disjoint cases::2+2=4::Every assignment belongs to exactly one P case.', 'Four satisfying assignments.', 'They are (F,T,F),(F,T,T),(T,F,T),(T,T,T), an explicit enumeration without duplicates.'),
E('trap', 'Do P→Q and Q→P have identical truth tables?', r'Choose an asymmetric row::P=T,\quad Q=F::A row with unequal inputs can distinguish implication directions.~Evaluate forward::T\to F=F::A true antecedent and false consequent violate the forward implication.~Evaluate reverse::F\to T=T::A false antecedent satisfies the reverse implication.', 'No; they disagree on this row and also on P=F,Q=T.', 'Their columns in TT,TF,FT,FF order are T,F,T,T and T,T,F,T.'))

P('0-propositions-implication', 'Implication', r'''
Read an implication as excluding a single forbidden combination: its premise holds but its promised consequence does not. This explains both its direction and its behavior when the premise fails. “P only if Q” places Q after the arrow because Q is necessary for P; “P if Q” places Q before it because Q is sufficient. For chained rules, track what becomes guaranteed after each true antecedent. To refute a proposed implication, produce one true antecedent with a false consequent. Material implication is a truth-functional mathematical convention; it does not assert causation or establish the antecedent itself. Negating an implication means asserting precisely its forbidden combination.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Translate “a candidate is eligible only if the candidate completed training,” then identify the violating case.', r'Name the statements::E=\text{eligible},\quad T=\text{trained}::Use separate symbols before deciding direction.~Translate necessity::E\to T::Training is required whenever eligibility holds.~Identify the failure::E=\mathrm{true},\quad T=\mathrm{false}::Being eligible without training is the single forbidden truth combination.', 'E→T; eligible and untrained violates the rule.', '¬E∨T has the same values and is false only in that violating case.'),
E('application', 'Given P→Q and Q→R, prove P→R.', r'Assume the target antecedent::P=T::Only assignments with true P could violate the desired implication.~Apply the first rule::P\to Q,\ P\Rightarrow Q::The first premise forces Q true.~Apply the second rule::Q\to R,\ Q\Rightarrow R::The second premise forces R true, so the target cannot fail.', 'P→R follows from the premises.', 'If P is false the target already holds; if P is true the chain forces R, covering both cases.'),
E('trap', 'Evaluate “if 9 is even, then 9 is prime” in classical propositional logic.', r'Evaluate the antecedent::::Nine is odd, so the antecedent is false.~Evaluate the consequent::::Nine equals three times three, so the consequent is false.~Apply implication::F\to F=T::A material implication fails only with a true antecedent and false consequent.', 'The implication is true, although both component claims are false.', 'Using ¬P∨Q gives true OR false, independently confirming the result.'))

P('0-propositions-equivalence-demorgan', 'Logical equivalence', r'''
Equivalence permits replacing a subexpression inside any larger propositional formula without altering truth values. Algebraic laws such as absorption and distributivity therefore function as proof steps, but each step must preserve every assignment. Keep equivalence separate from implication: simplifying a formula to a stronger sufficient condition can discard satisfying rows. A useful verification is to compare cases based on a variable rather than assume a familiar identity. In exam simplification, eliminate implications first, move negations inward with De Morgan’s laws, and then use identities or absorption. These transformations apply to classical Boolean formulas; ordinary arithmetic addition and multiplication do not obey exactly the same laws.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Simplify P∨(P∧Q) and prove the result.', r'Case true P::T\lor(T\land Q)=T::The first disjunct already makes the expression true.~Case false P::F\lor(F\land Q)=F::Both disjuncts are false regardless of Q.~Compare with P::P\lor(P\land Q)\equiv P::The two exhaustive cases reproduce P itself.', 'It simplifies to P by absorption.', 'The conjunction P∧Q never contributes a true row that P has not already contributed.'),
E('application', 'Find a conjunction equivalent to ¬(P→Q).', r'Eliminate the implication::\neg(P\to Q)\equiv\neg(\neg P\lor Q)::Replace P→Q by its equivalent disjunction.~Move negation inward::\neg(\neg P\lor Q)\equiv\neg\neg P\land\neg Q::De Morgan changes OR to AND and negates both operands.~Remove double negation::\neg\neg P\land\neg Q\equiv P\land\neg Q::Classical double negation restores P.', 'P∧¬Q.', 'Both formulas are true only when the implication fails: P true and Q false.'),
E('trap', 'Can P∨Q be replaced by P↔Q?', r'Choose unequal inputs::P=T,\quad Q=F::Equivalence must hold on every assignment, so one discriminating row suffices.~Evaluate OR::T\lor F=T::One true operand makes the disjunction true.~Evaluate biconditional::T\leftrightarrow F=F::A biconditional requires equal operand truth values.', 'No; the formulas are not logically equivalent.', 'On FF, OR is false but the biconditional is true, giving a second independent disagreement.'))

P('0-quantifiers-scope', 'First order logic', r'''
First order logic talks about objects and relations between objects, rather than treating every sentence as an indivisible Boolean input. Its interpretation supplies a domain, meanings for predicate symbols, and any functions or constants. Quantifiers range over that domain; restrictions such as “students” are normally expressed through predicates unless separate typed domains are stated. To translate a sentence, identify which objects are chosen universally, which witnesses may depend on them, and the scope of every condition. Universal restrictions usually use implication, whereas existential restrictions use conjunction. Evaluate a finite model by checking its actual predicate instances. The same formula can be true in one interpretation and false in another.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Using domains of people and exams, translate “every student passed some exam.” Let S(x) mean student and A(x,e) mean passed.', r'Choose each person::\forall x::The sentence considers every person in the people domain.~Restrict the requirement::S(x)\to\cdots::Only students must meet the passing condition; nonstudents are not forced to pass.~Allow a dependent exam witness::\forall x\bigl(S(x)\to\exists e\ A(x,e)\bigr)::Different students may have passed different exams.', '∀x(S(x)→∃e A(x,e)).', 'With two students passing different exams, the formula is true although one common exam witness need not exist.'),
E('application', 'In domain {1,2,3}, interpret R(x,y) as x<y. Is ∀x∃y R(x,y) true?', r'Test the first element::1<2::The value x=1 has a witness y=2.~Test the second element::2<3::The value x=2 has a witness y=3.~Test the largest element::3\not<1,\quad3\not<2,\quad3\not<3::No domain member is larger than three, so the universal requirement fails.', 'False in this finite interpretation.', 'Listing R={(1,2),(1,3),(2,3)} shows row three has no related target, independently exposing the failure.'),
E('trap', 'If the domain is empty, compare ∀x P(x) and ∃x P(x) under the standard empty-domain convention.', r'Test universal failure::::There is no domain object on which P can fail.~Evaluate the universal::\forall x\ P(x)=T::Universal truth is vacuous because there is no counterexample.~Evaluate the existential::\exists x\ P(x)=F::An existential needs an actual witness, and none exists.', 'The universal is true and the existential false.', 'This concerns the explicit empty-domain convention; it must not be inferred merely from absence of known objects.'))

P('0-quantifiers-predicate-satisfaction', 'Predicates', r'''
A predicate’s inputs have roles and domains, so arity and argument order are part of its meaning. A binary predicate R(x,y) may express divisibility, adjacency or precedence; swapping x and y can change its truth value. For a fixed assignment, evaluate the stated relation, not a related informal claim. Its satisfaction set can be described by solving a condition or by listing true tuples in a finite domain. Before evaluating an expression, ensure every operation is defined for the supplied input. A formula involving division by zero cannot be labeled false simply because its arithmetic is undefined. Domain restrictions turn such an expression into a well-defined predicate.
''',
E('fundamental', 'On D={1,2,3,4}, let P(x) mean that x is even. Find the satisfying inputs.', r'Use the definition::x=2k\text{ for an integer }k::An even number must have an integer half.~Check the members::2=2\cdot1,\quad4=2\cdot2::Two and four satisfy the condition; one and three do not.~Form the satisfaction set::\{x\in D:P(x)\}=\{2,4\}::Collect exactly the domain elements making the predicate true.', '{2,4}.', 'Evaluating the four instances gives F,T,F,T, matching the two listed inputs.'),
E('application', 'On positive integers, let R(x,y) mean x divides y. Evaluate R(2,4), R(4,2), and R(3,4).', r'Check the first ordered pair::4=2\cdot2::The target is an integer multiple of the first input, so R(2,4) is true.~Reverse the arguments::2/4=1/2\notin\mathbb Z::R(4,2) is false; argument order matters.~Check the third pair::4/3\notin\mathbb Z::Three does not divide four, so this instance is false.', 'True, false, false respectively.', 'The positive divisors of four are {1,2,4}; three is absent, while four is not a divisor of two.'),
E('trap', 'Can P(x): 1/x>0 be a total predicate on all real numbers without an extra convention?', r'Find the problematic input::x=0::Zero belongs to the proposed domain.~Check whether evaluation exists::1/0\text{ is undefined}::The expression does not yield a Boolean truth value at this input.~Repair the domain::D=\mathbb R\setminus\{0\}::Removing zero makes every input evaluable, and the satisfying inputs are then x>0.', 'No; restrict the domain or explicitly define the exceptional case.', 'On the repaired domain, negative inputs give a negative reciprocal and positive inputs give a positive reciprocal.'))

P('0-quantifiers-scope', 'Universal quantifier', r'''
A universal statement requires coverage of the whole stated domain, not just many successful samples. For an infinite numerical domain, an algebraic argument or exhaustive structural cases can cover every input at once. For a finite domain, checking every member is a complete proof. One failing member refutes the statement regardless of how many others pass. Quantified implications require attention to their antecedents: elements failing a restriction do not violate the implication. When negating a universal, preserve its domain and exhibit an existential counterexample to the inner condition. Nonempty-domain assumptions matter if existence is inferred from universality; universal truth alone does not supply an object.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Prove that for every integer n, n(n+1) is even.', r'Split integers by parity::n=2k\text{ or }n=2k+1::Every integer belongs to one of these exhaustive cases.~Handle even n::n(n+1)=2k(2k+1)::The product has a factor two.~Handle odd n::n(n+1)=(2k+1)2(k+1)::Now the consecutive factor is even, so this case also has a factor two.', 'The universal statement is true.', 'Two consecutive integers always have opposite parity, another complete argument rather than a list of examples.'),
E('application', 'Over D={−2,−1,0,1,2}, evaluate ∀x(x²≤2).', r'Locate a potential counterexample::x=2\in D::A universal can be refuted by any one allowed input.~Evaluate its square::2^2=4>2::The inner predicate fails on this domain member.~Negate the statement::\exists x\in D:\ x^2>2::The witness x=2 establishes the negation.', 'False.', 'The truth values at the five inputs are F,T,T,T,F; exhaustive evaluation confirms the counterexample.'),
E('trap', 'Does ∀x(S(x)→P(x)) imply that some student exists?', r'Consider no students::S(x)=F\text{ for every }x::This interpretation is permitted unless existence was assumed.~Evaluate every implication::F\to P(x)=T::All instances hold regardless of P.~Check existence::\exists x\ S(x)=F::The interpretation satisfies the universal but falsifies the existential.', 'No; the universal condition does not assert that students exist.', 'Adding ∃x S(x) would supply the missing existence assumption and would permit deriving ∃x P(x).'))

P('0-quantifiers-scope', 'Existential quantifier', r'''
An existential statement is proved by supplying a witness in the domain and verifying the entire required condition. A witness can depend on variables quantified earlier, but not on variables universally chosen later. To refute existence, rule out every candidate through a bound, contradiction, or exhaustive finite check. Be careful with conjunction inside the quantifier: the same witness must satisfy all conjuncts. Separate existential statements may use different witnesses, so their conjunction does not automatically combine into one witness satisfying everything. Algebraic construction is often more convincing than a few guesses. State the domain before choosing a witness, because an integer, real, or nonnegative restriction can change whether the construction works.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Over integers, prove ∃x(x²=49).', r'Construct a candidate::x=7\in\mathbb Z::The chosen value belongs to the stated domain.~Verify the predicate::7^2=49::It satisfies the full equation.~Conclude existence::\exists x\in\mathbb Z:\ x^2=49::One verified witness is enough; uniqueness is unnecessary.', 'True; x=7 is a witness.', 'The distinct witness x=−7 also works, showing existence does not mean exactly one solution.'),
E('application', 'Over real numbers, compare ∀x∃y(y>x) with ∃y∀x(y>x).', r'Construct a dependent witness::y=x+1>x::Every chosen x has a real value y that works.~Challenge a fixed witness::x=y+1::For any proposed common y, this is a permitted real x.~Evaluate the challenge::y<y+1=x::The fixed y fails the requirement y>x, ruling out every common witness.', 'The first statement is true; the second is false.', 'The real line has no greatest element, exactly the property used to defeat a fixed witness.'),
E('trap', 'On D={1,2}, P(x) means x=1 and Q(x) means x=2. Does (∃x P(x))∧(∃x Q(x)) imply ∃x(P(x)∧Q(x))?', r'Find the separate witnesses::P(1)=T,\quad Q(2)=T::The two existential statements are both true.~Demand one shared witness::x=1\land x=2::A single input would have to equal two different integers.~Rule out both candidates::P(1)\land Q(1)=F,\quad P(2)\land Q(2)=F::Neither domain element satisfies the conjunction.', 'No; separate witnesses cannot automatically be merged.', 'The satisfaction sets {1} and {2} are disjoint, so their intersection contains no possible common witness.'))

P('0-sets-functions-sets', 'Sets', r'''
Set operations can be verified through membership conditions before counting anything. An element belongs to a union if it passes at least one membership test, to an intersection if it passes both, and to a difference if it passes the first but fails the second. Complements require a fixed universe; changing it changes the answer. Nested sets require distinguishing an object from a subset containing that object. For finite sets, explicit enumeration gives an independent check of identities and power-set sizes. Remember that membership ignores repetition and order, whereas Cartesian products preserve coordinate order. Empty-set membership and empty-set inclusion answer different questions and therefore must be tested separately.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Let U={1,2,3,4,5}, A={1,3,5}, B={3,4}. Find A∖B and the complement of A in U.', r'Apply the difference condition::A\setminus B=\{1,5\}::Remove three from A because it also belongs to B.~Apply the complement condition::U\setminus A=\{2,4\}::Keep universe members absent from A.~Check disjointness::A\cap(U\setminus A)=\varnothing::An element cannot simultaneously belong and fail to belong to A.', 'A∖B={1,5}; Aᶜ={2,4}.', 'A∪Aᶜ={1,2,3,4,5}=U, confirming that the complement uses the stated universe.'),
E('application', 'A set A has four elements. How many subsets contain a fixed element a?', r'Fix the required membership::::The element a must appear, so it contributes no independent choice.~Choose the other members::2^3::Each of the other three elements may independently be included or omitted.~Count the subsets::2^3=8::All eight choices give distinct subsets containing a.', 'Eight subsets.', 'There are sixteen total subsets; pairing every subset with the result of toggling a divides them equally into containing and excluding a.'),
E('trap', 'For A={∅,{1}}, decide whether ∅∈A, ∅⊆A, and {1}⊆A.', r'Check empty-set membership::\varnothing\in A::The empty set is explicitly one of the two elements.~Check empty-set inclusion::\varnothing\subseteq A::There is no element of the empty set that could violate inclusion.~Check singleton inclusion::1\notin A\Rightarrow\{1\}\nsubseteq A::A contains the set {1}, not the number one itself.', 'True, true, false.', 'The statement {1}∈A is true, illustrating why membership of a set differs from inclusion of its members.'))

P('0-sets-functions-relations', 'Relations', r'''
A finite relation can be viewed as a directed edge set, an adjacency matrix, or a table of ordered pairs. Each representation encodes the same yes-or-no predicate, but property checks require the correct pattern. Reflexivity examines every diagonal pair; symmetry pairs each arrow with its reversal; antisymmetry permits arrows in both directions only on a loop. Transitivity checks every two-step route and demands its shortcut. Do not replace this universal route check with a claim that a diagram looks connected. A relation from A to B need not assign partners to every input and need not be single-valued. Those additional requirements belong to functions, not arbitrary relations.
''',
E('fundamental', 'On {1,2}, let R={(1,1),(2,2),(1,2)}. Is it reflexive, symmetric and antisymmetric?', r'Inspect the diagonal::(1,1),(2,2)\in R::Both required loops are present, so reflexivity holds.~Inspect reversal::(1,2)\in R,\quad(2,1)\notin R::The missing reversal disproves symmetry.~Inspect two-way distinct pairs::::There are no opposite arrows between different elements, so antisymmetry holds.', 'Reflexive and antisymmetric, but not symmetric.', 'The relation is the ordinary ≤ relation restricted to {1,2}, consistent with these properties.'),
E('application', 'On {a,b,c}, R={(a,b),(b,c)}. What pair must be added to make this relation transitive?', r'Find a two-step chain::aRb,\quad bRc::The middle endpoint b matches, so transitivity imposes a requirement.~Add its shortcut::(a,c)::This pair satisfies the only existing two-step route.~Recheck all chains::::No pair leaves c and none enters a, so the added shortcut creates no further missing two-step requirement.', 'Add (a,c); no other pair is necessary.', 'The transitive closure has exactly the three ordered pairs corresponding to reachability in the chain a→b→c.'),
E('trap', 'Can a relation be both symmetric and antisymmetric? Use the equality relation on {1,2,3}.', r'List equality pairs::R=\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\}::Equality includes only diagonal pairs.~Check symmetry::::Reversing a loop produces the same pair, so every reverse exists.~Check antisymmetry::::Whenever both directions hold, the endpoints are already equal.', 'Yes; equality is both symmetric and antisymmetric.', 'There are no distinct mutually related elements. Antisymmetric means a precise restriction, not merely “not symmetric.”'))

P('0-sets-functions-functions', 'Functions', r'''
The domain and codomain are part of a function’s specification, even when a familiar algebraic formula is used. Surjectivity compares the image with the declared codomain; injectivity compares outputs of distinct domain inputs. To prove an algebraic map injective, set two outputs equal and show their inputs must match. To prove surjectivity, start with an arbitrary codomain value and construct a permitted preimage. For a finite map, count or list preimages instead. Composition applies the inner map first, so a proposed inverse must return each input and each codomain value in the appropriate direction. Restricting the domain can remove collisions; enlarging the codomain can destroy surjectivity without changing the formula.
''',
E('fundamental', 'For f:{1,2,3}→{a,b,c} with f(1)=b,f(2)=c,f(3)=a, determine injectivity and surjectivity.', r'Compare outputs::::The three outputs b,c,a are all distinct, so no two domain inputs collide.~Compare image and codomain::f(\{1,2,3\})=\{a,b,c\}::Every declared target has a preimage.~Combine the properties::::The map is both injective and surjective, hence bijective.', 'Bijective.', 'The inverse assigns a→3,b→1,c→2 and recovers each original input.'),
E('application', 'Show that f:ℝ→ℝ, f(x)=3x−4, is bijective and find its inverse.', r'Prove injectivity::3x_1-4=3x_2-4\Rightarrow x_1=x_2::Equal outputs force equal inputs.~Construct every preimage::y=3x-4\Rightarrow x=(y+4)/3::This real input exists for every real codomain value.~Write the inverse::f^{-1}(y)=\frac{y+4}{3}::The preimage is unique, so the inverse is a function.', 'Bijective; f⁻¹(y)=(y+4)/3.', 'Composing gives 3((y+4)/3)−4=y and ((3x−4)+4)/3=x.'),
E('trap', 'Is f:ℝ→ℝ, f(x)=x², a bijection?', r'Find an input collision::f(1)=f(-1)=1::Different real inputs share an output, so injectivity fails.~Find an unattained target::x^2\ne-1\text{ for real }x::The negative codomain value −1 has no preimage, so surjectivity fails.~Distinguish a repaired specification::::Restricting both domain and codomain to nonnegative reals yields a bijection, but that is a different function specification.', 'No; it is neither injective nor surjective as stated.', 'Its image is [0,∞), and every positive image value has two real preimages.'))

P('0-sets-functions-relations', 'Equivalence relations', r'''
An equivalence relation formalizes sameness with respect to one selected feature, even when the objects themselves differ. Reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity jointly ensure that choosing a different representative does not alter an equivalence class. To construct classes on a finite set, place related elements together, then check that the resulting blocks are disjoint and cover the domain. Conversely, a partition defines an equivalence relation by declaring two elements related exactly when they share a block. A relation that groups some similar-looking pairs without transitivity may fail to produce genuine classes. In modular examples, residues determine the feature of sameness; the difference of related integers must be divisible by the modulus.
''',
E('fundamental', 'On {0,1,2,3,4,5}, define aRb when a≡b (mod 3). List the equivalence classes.', r'Use remainder zero::[0]=\{0,3\}::These elements differ from zero by a multiple of three.~Use the other remainders::[1]=\{1,4\},\quad[2]=\{2,5\}::The other two residue values form their own blocks.~Check coverage::::The blocks are disjoint and together contain all six elements, so they are the complete classes.', '{0,3}, {1,4}, {2,5}.', 'Every within-block difference is divisible by three; every cross-block difference has a nonzero remainder.'),
E('application', 'A six-element set is partitioned into blocks of sizes 3,2,1. How many ordered pairs are in the induced equivalence relation?', r'Count within the first block::3^2=9::Every first element can be paired with every element in its own three-member block.~Count the other blocks::2^2+1^2=5::The same Cartesian-product rule applies independently within each block.~Add disjoint pair sets::9+4+1=14::No related pair crosses between blocks.', 'Fourteen ordered pairs.', 'The six diagonal pairs are included; off-diagonal counts 3·2+2·1+1·0=8 give the same total 14.'),
E('trap', 'On {1,2,3}, define aRb when |a−b|≤1. Is this an equivalence relation?', r'Check easy axioms::|a-a|=0,\quad|a-b|=|b-a|::The relation is reflexive and symmetric.~Find a chain::1R2,\quad2R3::Both adjacent differences equal one.~Test the shortcut::|1-3|=2>1::The required pair 1R3 fails, disproving transitivity.', 'No; transitivity fails.', 'The supposed neighborhoods [1]={1,2} and [2]={1,2,3} overlap without being identical, which genuine equivalence classes cannot do.'))

P('0-orders-lattices-posets', 'Partial orders', r'''
Partial orders compare objects through a specified relation, so ordinary numerical size may be irrelevant. Divisibility and inclusion allow some pairs to remain incomparable while still satisfying reflexivity, antisymmetry and transitivity. To identify a least element, test whether it lies below every object; being minimal only excludes a strictly smaller object. These conditions differ precisely because incomparability is permitted. The dual distinction holds for greatest and maximal elements. On a finite set, listing each object’s predecessors and successors can settle the questions without relying on a sketch. A strict order deliberately removes equality and therefore is not reflexive; it must not be checked against the non-strict partial-order axioms unchanged.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Under inclusion on {∅,{a},{b},{a,b}}, are {a} and {b} comparable? Identify least and greatest elements.', r'Test both inclusions::\{a\}\nsubseteq\{b\},\quad\{b\}\nsubseteq\{a\}::Each singleton contains an element absent from the other.~Find a universal lower element::\varnothing\subseteq X\text{ for every }X::The empty set is least.~Find a universal upper element::X\subseteq\{a,b\}\text{ for every }X::The full set is greatest.', 'The singletons are incomparable; ∅ is least and {a,b} greatest.', 'Reflexivity, antisymmetry and transitivity all follow directly from membership inclusion.'),
E('application', 'On {2,3,6} ordered by divisibility, find minimal elements and determine whether a least element exists.', r'Inspect predecessors of two and three::::Neither has a distinct divisor inside this set, so both are minimal.~Compare the minimal elements::2\nmid3,\quad3\nmid2::Neither lies below the other, so neither can be least.~Inspect six::2\mid6,\quad3\mid6::Six is greatest, but it cannot provide a missing least element.', 'Minimal elements are 2 and 3; there is no least element.', 'The relation contains the three diagonal pairs and (2,6),(3,6), with no candidate below all three.'),
E('trap', 'Is the relation aRb iff a<b on {1,2,3} a non-strict partial order?', r'Test reflexivity::1\not<1::A non-strict partial order requires every self-pair.~Check the other familiar properties::::Strict numerical order is transitive and has no two-way distinct comparisons.~Apply the actual definition::::Failure of reflexivity suffices even though the other properties look order-like.', 'No; it is a strict order, not a reflexive partial-order relation.', 'Replacing < by ≤ supplies all three diagonal pairs and yields the corresponding non-strict partial order.'))

P('0-orders-lattices-posets', 'Hasse diagrams', r'''
A Hasse diagram stores only cover relations, making it a compressed representation of a finite partial order. A cover has no intermediate object between its endpoints; any comparison recoverable through an upward path is omitted as an edge. Recovering the full relation therefore requires reflexive pairs and transitive reachability. Edge counts in the drawing are not counts of all ordered comparable pairs. For inclusion, covers add one element; for divisibility within a restricted domain, covers depend on which intermediate divisors are present. Verify an edge by checking the stated order and the absence of an intermediate element, rather than by geometric closeness. Vertical placement records order direction, while horizontal placement has no algebraic meaning.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Find the Hasse edges for divisibility on {1,2,4}.', r'Identify direct comparisons::1\mid2,\quad2\mid4,\quad1\mid4::All three strict comparisons hold.~Remove the non-cover::1\mid2\mid4::The intermediate divisor two prevents 1–4 from being a cover.~Keep the covers::::Draw 1 below 2 below 4, with edges 1–2 and 2–4.', 'A three-element chain with two drawn edges.', 'Its upward paths recover all three strict comparisons, including the omitted 1≤4 relation.'),
E('application', 'How many Hasse edges does the power set of {a,b,c}, ordered by inclusion, have?', r'Count bottom-level covers::\binom30\cdot3=3::The empty set can acquire any one element.~Count middle-level covers::\binom31\cdot2=6::Each singleton has two possible one-element extensions.~Count top-level covers::\binom32\cdot1=3::Each two-element subset has one extension to the full set, giving twelve edges total.', 'Twelve edges.', 'Each of three elements can be the added element while the other two have four membership choices: 3·2²=12.'),
E('trap', 'A Hasse drawing is a chain a below b below c. Does its order relation have only the two drawn pairs?', r'Include reflexivity::(a,a),(b,b),(c,c)::Every object is related to itself even though no loop is drawn.~Include drawn covers::(a,b),(b,c)::These are genuine strict comparisons.~Include upward reachability::(a,c)::Transitivity restores the comparison represented by the two-edge path.', 'No; the full relation has six ordered pairs.', 'A three-element total order has n(n+1)/2=6 non-strict ordered comparisons.'))

P('0-orders-lattices-lattices', 'Lattices', r'''
A lattice makes two binary operations available from order alone: meet selects the greatest common lower bound and join the least common upper bound. Existence of some bound does not prove that the required extremal bound exists. In a finite poset, collect all common bounds, then check whether one compares correctly with all the others. In familiar lattices, domain closure remains essential: the gcd or union computed algebraically must actually belong to the poset. Meet and join need not equal numerical minimum and maximum because the order may be divisibility or inclusion. Counting join outcomes in a power-set lattice can be done element by element, using independent membership choices.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Among positive divisors of 18 ordered by divisibility, find 6∧9 and 6∨9.', r'Find common lower bounds::\gcd(6,9)=3::Every common divisor divides three, which itself divides both inputs.~Find common upper bounds::\operatorname{lcm}(6,9)=18::Eighteen is divisible by both and divides every common multiple.~Check the domain::::Three and eighteen are divisors of eighteen, so both bounds belong to this lattice.', 'Meet 3; join 18.', 'The divisor set is {1,2,3,6,9,18}; common lower bounds are {1,3}, and the only common upper bound is 18.'),
E('application', 'In the power-set lattice of a three-element set U, how many ordered pairs (A,B) have join U?', r'Translate the join::A\vee B=A\cup B=U::Every universe element must occur in at least one of the two sets.~Count local memberships::::Each element can be in A only, B only, or both; absence from both is forbidden.~Multiply independent choices::3^3=27::Choosing a state for each of three distinct elements uniquely specifies an ordered pair.', 'Twenty-seven ordered pairs.', 'Summing over A gives Σₖ C(3,k)2ᵏ=(1+2)³=27 because B must contain U∖A and may choose freely within A.'),
E('trap', 'A poset has bottom 0, top 1, and a,b below both c,d, with c,d incomparable and a,b incomparable. Does having top and bottom make it a lattice?', r'Find upper bounds of a and b::::The common upper bounds are c,d,1.~Inspect the minimal upper bounds::::Neither c nor d lies below the other, so neither is least among all upper bounds.~Reject the top as a join::::One lies above c and d rather than below them, so it is not the least upper bound.', 'No; a and b have no join.', 'Adding a new element below both c,d and above both a,b would repair this particular missing join; top and bottom alone do not.'))

P('0-algebraic-structures-monoid-concatenation', 'Monoids', r'''
Monoids model repeatable composition with a neutral action. Their operation may act on strings, numbers or transformations; the axioms concern the specified operation rather than the objects' appearance. Associativity permits long expressions to be evaluated without selecting parentheses, while an identity handles the case of composing no actions. Closure must hold for every pair of allowed members. In a transformation monoid, composition order matters and many transformations may lose information, so inverses are optional. To reject a monoid, one counterexample to closure or associativity, or proof that no two-sided identity exists, suffices. A right identity alone cannot replace the required two-sided identity.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Do nonnegative integers under addition form a monoid?', r'Check closure::a,b\ge0\Rightarrow a+b\ge0::Adding two allowed integers remains allowed.~Check associativity::(a+b)+c=a+(b+c)::Integer addition does not depend on grouping.~Identify the identity::a+0=0+a=a::Zero belongs to the set and acts neutrally on both sides.', 'Yes; the identity is zero.', 'The positive number one lacks an additive inverse in this set, showing that this monoid need not be a group.'),
E('application', 'Consider all functions from {0,1} to itself under composition. How many members are there, and do they form a monoid?', r'Count the functions::2^2=4::Each of the two inputs independently chooses one of two outputs.~Check closure and grouping::::Composing total self-maps remains a total self-map, and function composition is associative.~Find the neutral function::\operatorname{id}(x)=x::Composing with the identity map on either side preserves every function.', 'Four members forming a monoid.', 'The members are identity, swap, constant zero and constant one; the constant maps are noninvertible but remain legitimate monoid members.'),
E('trap', 'Do integers with operation a*b=a−b form a monoid because a*0=a?', r'Check the other identity side::0*a=-a::Zero fails to preserve nonzero inputs on the left.~Compare groupings::(5*3)*1=1,\quad5*(3*1)=3::Subtraction is not associative.~Apply the definition::::A right-neutral element cannot compensate for failed associativity and absence of a two-sided identity.', 'No.', 'If a universal left identity e existed, e−a=a would force e=2a for every integer a, which is impossible.'))

P('0-algebraic-structures-groups', 'Groups', r'''
A group permits every allowed operation to be undone, so cancellation and reversible composition become valid. The inverse must remain inside the same set, and the identity belongs to that set too. Finite groups impose arithmetic restrictions: an element’s order and a subgroup’s size divide the group size. These are necessary conditions rather than promises that every divisor occurs as an element order. Modular multiplication gives groups only after restricting to residues coprime to the modulus; nonzero residues suffice when the modulus is prime. To test a claimed group, first inspect closure and identity, then associativity and inverses. Commutativity is an additional property, not a required group axiom.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Under multiplication modulo 5, find the order of 2 in {1,2,3,4}.', r'Compute successive powers::2^1\equiv2,\quad2^2\equiv4\pmod5::Neither returns the identity one.~Continue the powers::2^3\equiv3,\quad2^4\equiv1\pmod5::The first positive return occurs at exponent four.~Identify the generated group::::The powers visit all four nonzero residues, which form a group modulo the prime five.', 'Element 2 has order four and generates the group.', 'Its inverse is three because 2·3≡1; the order four divides the group’s size four.'),
E('application', 'Can a finite group of order 18 have a subgroup of order 5?', r'Apply the coset partition::::Every left coset of a subgroup has the same size as that subgroup.~Use divisibility::|H|\mid|G|::Disjoint equal-sized cosets partition a finite group.~Check the candidate::5\nmid18::The proposed subgroup size cannot partition eighteen elements.', 'No.', 'Eighteen divided by five is not an integer, whereas the number of cosets must be an integer.'),
E('trap', 'Do all nonzero residues modulo 8 form a multiplication group?', r'Test two allowed inputs::2,4\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7\}::Both are nonzero residues.~Multiply them::2\cdot4\equiv0\pmod8::The output is excluded from the proposed set.~Reject closure::::Failure of closure rules out a group before any inverse argument is needed.', 'No.', 'Restricting instead to the units {1,3,5,7} gives a closed group; each unit squares to one modulo eight.'))

P('0-algebraic-structures-identity-custom-operation', 'Identity', r'''
The neutral element is determined by equations valid for every allowed input. For an operation with one-sided behavior, solve the left and right equations separately; their solutions may differ or one may not exist. In an ordered operation such as minimum or maximum, the domain’s extremal elements determine whether an identity is available. A finite domain can therefore have an identity that disappears when the same formula is used over an unbounded domain. If a two-sided identity exists, it is unique: composing two proposed identities returns each, forcing equality. A left identity alone may be nonunique, so applying uniqueness without the two-sided hypothesis is a mistake.
''',
E('fundamental', 'For a*b=min(a,b) on {1,2,3,4,5}, find the identity.', r'Write the requirement::\min(a,e)=a\text{ for every }a::The candidate must be at least every domain member.~Use the largest member::\min(5,e)=5\Rightarrow e=5::The only permitted value large enough is five.~Check both sides::\min(a,5)=\min(5,a)=a::Commutativity and the domain bound verify a two-sided identity.', 'Five.', 'For e<5, using a=5 produces e rather than five, proving uniqueness within the domain.'),
E('application', 'Can max(a,b) on all integers have an identity?', r'Write the neutral condition::\max(a,e)=a::A putative identity must never exceed any input.~Challenge a candidate::a=e-1\in\mathbb Z::The integer domain always contains a value below e.~Evaluate the challenge::\max(e-1,e)=e\ne e-1::Every proposed integer identity fails on this permitted input.', 'No integer identity exists.', 'On a finite integer interval, its minimum would be an identity for max; the missing lower bound explains the failure here.'),
E('trap', 'On a set with at least two elements, define a*b=b. Is every element a two-sided identity?', r'Check left neutrality::e*a=a::Every e works on the left because the output is the second input.~Check right neutrality::a*e=e::The output equals e, not an arbitrary a.~Choose a distinct input::a\ne e\Rightarrow a*e\ne a::No e works for every input on the right.', 'Every element is a left identity, but no two-sided identity exists.', 'The operation is associative, so this example isolates the missing two-sided identity rather than a grouping failure.'))

P('0-algebraic-structures-inverse-modular', 'Inverses', r'''
An inverse reverses the operation relative to its own identity: additive and multiplicative inverses of the same residue are generally different. Modular multiplicative inverses can be found by Bézout coefficients when the modulus and input are coprime. Additive inverses exist for every residue because subtracting the residue always returns zero modulo the modulus. Check the defining product or sum after computing a candidate; this catches accidental use of the wrong identity. Inverses in a group are unique, and the inverse of a product reverses factor order. Outside a group, one-sided inverses and excluded-domain candidates need care. Zero cannot have a multiplicative inverse when zero and one are distinct.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Find the additive inverse of 4 modulo 9.', r'Use the additive identity::4+b\equiv0\pmod9::The desired operation is addition, so its neutral value is zero.~Solve for the residue::b\equiv-4\equiv5\pmod9::Reduce the negative value to an allowed representative.~Verify::4+5=9\equiv0\pmod9::The sum reproduces the identity.', 'Five modulo nine.', 'Reversing the addition gives the same result; a multiplicative inverse would instead have to yield one.'),
E('application', 'Use Euclidean back-substitution to find the multiplicative inverse of 7 modulo 17.', r'Compute remainders::17=2\cdot7+3,\quad7=2\cdot3+1::The gcd is one, so an inverse exists.~Back substitute::1=7-2(17-2\cdot7)=5\cdot7-2\cdot17::The coefficient of seven supplies the inverse modulo seventeen.~Reduce and check::7\cdot5=35\equiv1\pmod{17}::Five is already a standard residue representative.', 'Seven’s inverse is five modulo seventeen.', 'Multiplying thirty-five subtracts two full moduli and leaves one, an independent direct verification.'),
E('trap', 'In real-number multiplication, can zero have an inverse?', r'Write the inverse equation::0b=1::Multiplicative inversion must return identity one.~Use the zero-product rule::0b=0\text{ for every real }b::No candidate changes the product.~Compare required and actual outputs::0\ne1::The equation has no solution, so the inverse does not exist.', 'No.', 'A reciprocal 1/0 is undefined for exactly this reason; zero’s additive inverse is zero, which is a different operation.'))

P('0-graphs-matching-connectivity', 'Graphs', r'''
Select the graph model before applying a counting rule. A simple undirected graph has neither loops nor repeated edges; each ordinary edge contributes one to the degree of each endpoint. This makes the degree sum twice the edge count, but parity alone cannot certify that a proposed degree sequence is realizable. In complete bipartite graphs, every edge crosses between the two parts, so edge counting is a product rather than a choice of arbitrary vertex pairs. Directed graphs instead have separate incoming and outgoing totals. A useful check combines a global count with local constraints: a vertex cannot have more than n−1 neighbors in a simple n-vertex graph, and high-degree vertices impose demands on other degrees.
''',
E('fundamental', 'A simple undirected graph has degrees 3,3,2,2,2. How many edges does it have?', r'Sum the degrees::3+3+2+2+2=12::Count all incident edge ends.~Apply handshaking::\sum_v\deg(v)=2|E|::Each undirected edge was counted at both endpoints.~Solve the edge count::|E|=12/2=6::Divide the total by two.', 'Six edges.', 'A five-cycle with one added chord has degree sequence 3,3,2,2,2 and six edges, confirming feasibility as well as the count.'),
E('application', 'For the complete bipartite graph K₃,₄, find its edge count and the degrees on each side.', r'Count crossing pairs::|E|=3\cdot4=12::Every vertex on the first side joins every vertex on the second.~Read first-side degrees::\deg(x)=4::Each of the three vertices has all four opposite neighbors.~Read second-side degrees::\deg(y)=3::Each of the four vertices has three opposite neighbors.', 'Twelve edges; degrees four on the three-vertex side and three on the four-vertex side.', 'The degree sum is 3·4+4·3=24=2·12; edges within either part are absent.'),
E('trap', 'Is 3,3,1,1 a possible degree sequence for a simple graph on four vertices merely because its sum is even?', r'Use the first degree-three vertex::::It must connect to every other vertex.~Use the second degree-three vertex::::It also connects to both proposed degree-one vertices.~Inspect the small degrees::\deg(v)\ge2::Each of those vertices already has two distinct neighbors, contradicting degree one.', 'No; even degree sum is necessary but insufficient.', 'The two universal vertices force at least the five edges between them and all remaining vertices, already giving total degree at least ten rather than eight.'))

P('0-graphs-matching-connectivity', 'Connectivity', r'''
Connectivity concerns existence of paths, not just the number or individual degrees of edges. A traversal from one vertex reaches exactly its component; restarting from an unreached vertex finds another component. Removing a vertex or edge can split a component, which makes articulation points and bridges natural applications. To join k existing components, each new edge can reduce the component count by at most one, so at least k−1 edges are needed; connecting representatives in a chain attains that bound. These statements assume undirected graphs. In a directed graph, reachability in one direction is weaker than strong connectivity, which demands routes both ways between every ordered pair.
''',
E('fundamental', 'The path A–B–C–D loses vertex B and its incident edges. How many components remain?', r'Update the edge set::::Deleting B removes AB and BC; only CD remains.~Traverse the surviving edge::::C and D form one connected component.~Count the isolated survivor::::A remains a vertex with no edge, creating a second component.', 'Two components: {A} and {C,D}.', 'The remaining forest has three vertices and one edge, so components=V−E=2.'),
E('application', 'An undirected network has four connected components. What is the minimum number of edges needed to make it connected?', r'Bound improvement per edge::::An edge between different components merges only those two, reducing the count by one.~Obtain a lower bound::4-1=3::Three component reductions are necessary.~Construct a matching upper bound::::Choose one representative from each component and connect the four representatives in a chain with three edges.', 'Three new edges.', 'After each chain edge the component counts are 3,2,1; hence the lower bound is attained.'),
E('trap', 'A simple graph has four vertices and three edges. Must it be connected because E=V−1?', r'Construct a counterexample::::Take a triangle on three vertices and leave the fourth isolated.~Check the edge count::|E|=3=4-1::The numerical condition is satisfied.~Check components::::The triangle and isolated vertex are separate components, so connectivity fails.', 'No; E=V−1 alone does not imply a tree or connectivity.', 'The same example contains a cycle. Connectedness plus this edge count would force a tree, but the missing assumption matters.'))

P('0-graphs-matching-matching', 'Matching', r'''
A matching assigns disjoint pairs, so an edge’s endpoints cannot appear in another selected edge. Maximum size is a global optimization condition; maximality only says the current selection cannot be extended by adding one edge. A perfect matching covers every vertex and therefore requires an even vertex count. For a bipartite graph, Hall’s condition checks competition for partners: every subset of one side must collectively have enough neighbors. Checking each vertex separately misses bottlenecks shared by several vertices. Upper bounds such as half the vertex count help certify optimality when a construction attains them. An augmenting path can explain how rearrangement improves a maximal matching without ever using overlapping edges in the final result.
''',
E('fundamental', 'What is the maximum matching size in a triangle?', r'Bound the number of disjoint edges::2|M|\le3::Every selected edge consumes two distinct vertices.~Use integrality::|M|\le1::An integer matching size cannot exceed one and a half.~Construct a matching::::Select any one triangle edge; it attains the bound.', 'Maximum size one; no perfect matching exists.', 'Every pair of distinct triangle edges shares a vertex, independently excluding a size-two matching.'),
E('application', 'A bipartite graph has X={a,b,c}, Y={1,2,3}, with edges a1,b1,c2,c3. Can a matching saturate X?', r'Choose a competing subset::S=\{a,b\}::Both vertices seek partners from the same restricted neighborhood.~Compute its neighbors::N(S)=\{1\}::Only one target is available for the two vertices.~Apply Hall’s obstruction::|N(S)|=1<2=|S|::The subset cannot receive distinct partners, so saturation is impossible.', 'No matching saturates X.', 'A size-two matching such as {a1,c2} exists; a and b cannot both be covered, proving the maximum is two.'),
E('trap', 'In the six-cycle 1–2–3–4–5–6–1, is matching {1–2,4–5} maximum because no edge can be added?', r'Find the unmatched vertices::::Only vertices three and six remain unmatched, and they are not adjacent.~Confirm maximality::::Every unused edge touches a selected edge, so none can be added directly.~Construct a larger matching::\{1\! -\!2,3\! -\!4,5\! -\!6\}::These three disjoint edges cover all six vertices.', 'It is maximal but not maximum; the maximum has size three.', 'The vertex-count bound is six divided by two, and the displayed perfect matching attains it.'))

P('0-graphs-matching-colouring', 'Colouring', r'''
A proper vertex colouring gives adjacent vertices different colours while allowing nonadjacent vertices to share one. To determine a chromatic number, prove a lower bound and provide a colouring meeting it. Cliques give lower bounds because all their vertices conflict pairwise. Bipartitions give two-colour constructions when no edge stays within one part. Maximum degree supplies some general bounds but does not itself equal the chromatic number. Odd cycles obstruct two-colouring because alternating colours returns the wrong colour at the closing edge. An explicit assignment is an essential check: a proposed colour count without a valid construction is only a claim. All examples here use finite simple undirected graphs and vertex colouring.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Find the chromatic number of the path 1–2–3–4–5.', r'Get a lower bound::::At least one edge exists, so one colour is insufficient.~Assign colours by parity::::Colour vertices 1,3,5 red and 2,4 blue.~Check every edge::::Each edge joins consecutive indices of opposite parity, so the assignment is proper.', 'Two.', 'The two colour classes form a bipartition and jointly cover all vertices without an internal edge.'),
E('application', 'Start with K₄ on A,B,C,D and remove edge CD. Find its chromatic number.', r'Find a clique::\{A,B,C\}\text{ forms }K_3::These three pairwise adjacent vertices require three colours.~Exploit the missing edge::::Assign C and D the same colour, because they are the only nonadjacent pair.~Colour the other vertices::::Give A and B two distinct additional colours; all remaining edges join different colours.', 'Three.', 'The clique lower bound and explicit three-colour construction coincide, certifying the exact answer.'),
E('trap', 'Does a cycle with maximum degree two always need only two colours? Test the five-cycle.', r'Attempt alternation::::Starting red,blue,red,blue forces the fifth vertex red.~Check the closing edge::::The fifth vertex is adjacent to the first red vertex, so two colours fail.~Construct three colours::::Use red,blue,red,blue,green around the cycle; every neighboring pair differs.', 'No; the five-cycle has chromatic number three.', 'Any two-colouring must alternate along each edge, so an odd number of steps cannot return consistently to the starting colour.'))

P('0-counting-choices', 'Counting', r'''
Counting begins by defining what makes two outcomes distinct. Sequential decisions use multiplication only after the number of choices available at each stage has been determined, including dependence on earlier choices. Alternative cases use addition only when those cases are disjoint; overlapping alternatives need correction. A useful strategy is to partition by the first decision that changes the allowed choices. Restrictions on leading zeros, repeated symbols or role assignments should be incorporated before multiplying. To verify a count, enumerate a small analogous case or count the same outcome set through a different partition. These principles assume finite outcome sets and count possibilities, not probabilities; unequal likelihood does not change the number of outcomes.
''',
E('fundamental', 'How many four-digit PIN strings are possible if leading zero and repeated digits are allowed?', r'Count each position::10\text{ choices per position}::Every position can use any decimal digit.~Check independence of choices::::Using a digit does not remove it from later positions.~Multiply::10^4=10000::Each sequence of four choices produces exactly one PIN string.', 'Ten thousand.', 'The strings run from 0000 through 9999, giving a second direct count.'),
E('application', 'A five-character identifier is either two uppercase letters followed by three digits, or one uppercase letter followed by four digits. Repetition is allowed. Count identifiers.', r'Count the first format::26^2\cdot10^3=676000::The letter and digit choices are independent.~Count the second format::26\cdot10^4=260000::This format has one letter position and four digit positions.~Add disjoint formats::676000+260000=936000::The second character is a letter in the first format and a digit in the second, so no identifier is counted twice.', '936,000 identifiers.', 'Partitioning by whether the second character is a letter or digit yields the same two disjoint cases.'),
E('trap', 'How many two-digit positive integers have distinct digits? Explain why 10·9 is incorrect.', r'Restrict the leading digit::9\text{ choices}::A positive two-digit integer cannot start with zero.~Restrict the second digit::9\text{ choices}::Zero is now allowed, but the already chosen leading digit is excluded.~Multiply::9\cdot9=81::Both restrictions are enforced in the stage counts.', 'Eighty-one.', 'There are ninety two-digit integers from 10 through 99; excluding 11,22,…,99 leaves 90−9=81.'))

P('0-counting-choices', 'Permutations', r'''
Permutations count arrangements in which order, position or assigned role matters. For a selection of r distinct objects from n without replacement, the successive choices decrease and yield n! divided by (n−r)!. Repeated-looking objects require dividing by the number of indistinguishable reorderings, provided that every arrangement has the same multiplicity. Circular arrangements introduce a different equivalence: rotating everybody together may preserve the outcome if seats are unlabeled. Fixing one object removes that rotational repetition. Reflection should be identified only when the problem explicitly considers mirror images identical. Translate these assumptions into the outcome definition before applying a formula, because the same people can produce different counts under different notions of sameness.
''',
E('fundamental', 'How many ordered lists of three distinct students can be selected from six students?', r'Choose the first entry::6::Any student may lead the list.~Choose later entries::5\cdot4::Previously selected students cannot be repeated.~Multiply the stages::6\cdot5\cdot4=120::Each sequence corresponds to one distinct ordered list.', '120 lists.', 'C(6,3)·3!=20·6=120 independently separates selecting members from arranging them.'),
E('application', 'How many distinct arrangements of the letters in BANANA are possible?', r'Count labeled arrangements::6!=720::Temporarily treat all six letter occurrences as distinct.~Identify invisible reorderings::3!\cdot2!::Permuting the three As or the two Ns does not change the visible word.~Divide equal multiplicities::\frac{6!}{3!2!}=60::Every visible arrangement has exactly that many labeled versions.', 'Sixty arrangements.', 'Choosing three A positions, then two N positions among the remaining three, gives C(6,3)C(3,2)=20·3=60.'),
E('trap', 'Seat four distinct people around an unlabeled circular table. Rotations are identical but mirror orders differ. Is the count 4!?', r'Remove rotations::::Fix one named person as a reference position.~Arrange the others::3!=6::Their clockwise order uniquely specifies an arrangement relative to the reference.~Preserve reflections::::Clockwise and counterclockwise orders remain different under the stated assumption.', 'Six, not twenty-four.', 'Every circular arrangement has four labeled-seat rotations; 4!/4=6. No extra division by two is justified.'))

P('0-counting-unordered-committee', 'Combinations', r'''
An unordered selection is determined by membership rather than the order in which the members were chosen. This permits combinations, but constraints still require care. When selecting specified numbers from disjoint categories, choose independently within each category and multiply. When repetitions of types are allowed, ordinary subsets no longer describe the outcome because a type may occur more than once; count multisets instead. Complementary selection gives the identity C(n,r)=C(n,n−r), which often supplies a useful numerical check. State whether objects are distinguishable and whether replacement is allowed. A committee with assigned offices returns to ordered counting, whereas a committee with only membership uses a subset count.
''',
E('fundamental', 'How many unordered pairs can be chosen from six distinct books without repetition?', r'Count ordered picks::6\cdot5=30::Temporarily distinguish first and second selections.~Find pair multiplicity::2!=2::Each unordered pair appears in both possible orders.~Remove order::\binom62=30/2=15::Division gives every two-book subset exactly once.', 'Fifteen pairs.', 'Listing pairs by the earlier book gives 5+4+3+2+1=15.'),
E('application', 'Select a team with exactly two of four seniors and one of three juniors. Members have no roles. Count teams.', r'Choose the senior subset::\binom42=6::The two seniors are unordered and distinct.~Choose the junior::\binom31=3::Exactly one junior is needed.~Combine disjoint categories::6\cdot3=18::Every senior pair can accompany each junior, with no overlap of categories.', 'Eighteen teams.', 'Partitioning teams by their junior gives three classes, each containing the six senior pairs.'),
E('trap', 'Choose two scoops from three ice-cream flavors, with repetition allowed and scoop order ignored. Does C(3,2) apply?', r'Allow repeated flavors::::The outcomes AA,BB,CC are legal and ordinary two-element subsets omit them.~List the mixed outcomes::::AB,AC,BC give the three selections using distinct flavors.~Combine the two classes::3+3=6::The repeated and mixed selections are disjoint.', 'Six selections, not three.', 'The multiset formula C(3+2−1,2)=C(4,2)=6 agrees with the complete list.'))

P('0-counting-inclusion-exclusion-three-sets', 'Inclusion–exclusion', r'''
Inclusion–exclusion works by fixing the contribution of one object according to how many conditions it satisfies. Singleton counts add it once per condition, pair counts remove repeated memberships, and higher intersections restore overcorrections with alternating signs. The formula assumes intersection counts include all objects satisfying those conditions, even if additional conditions also hold. “Exactly two” regions therefore must be converted before entering a standard three-set formula. For restrictions such as fixed points of permutations, define a bad-event set for each position and count intersections by satisfying all the selected requirements. Checking the final number against the universe size and examining disjoint regions helps detect inconsistent or misinterpreted input counts.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Among integers from 1 through 30, how many are divisible by 2 or 3?', r'Count the individual sets::\lfloor30/2\rfloor=15,\quad\lfloor30/3\rfloor=10::Each quotient counts the multiples in the stated range.~Count the overlap::\lfloor30/6\rfloor=5::Divisibility by both means divisibility by their least common multiple six.~Correct double counting::15+10-5=20::Subtract each overlap member once.', 'Twenty integers.', 'The complement consists of 1,5,7,11,13,17,19,23,25,29: ten integers, leaving twenty in the union.'),
E('application', 'How many permutations of four distinct objects leave no object in its original position?', r'Define bad fixed-point events::::Fixing any chosen k positions leaves (4−k)! permutations of the remaining objects.~Count alternating corrections::4!-\binom41 3!+\binom42 2!-\binom43 1!+\binom44 0!::Choose which positions are fixed and use inclusion–exclusion on their union.~Evaluate::24-24+12-4+1=9::The final correction includes the unique permutation fixing all four positions.', 'Nine derangements.', 'Cycle structures are either one four-cycle, giving six, or two disjoint transpositions, giving three; total nine.'),
E('trap', 'Sets A,B,C have sizes 40,35,20. Exactly AB-only has 8 members, AC-only 6, BC-only 4, and the triple intersection 3. Find the union.', r'Convert to inclusive intersections::|A\cap B|=11,\quad|A\cap C|=9,\quad|B\cap C|=7::Each pair intersection also includes the three triple members.~Apply the standard formula::40+35+20-11-9-7+3=71::Use inclusive overlaps rather than exactly-two regions.~Check disjoint single regions::23+20+7=50::Subtract the appropriate exactly-two and triple counts from each individual set.', 'Seventy-one members.', 'The disjoint regions sum to 50+8+6+4+3=71. Substituting 8,6,4 as inclusive counts would incorrectly retain extra triple contributions.'))

P('0-counting-overlap', 'Stars and bars', r'''
Stars and bars represents an allocation of identical units to labeled bins by a row of stars separated into groups. For nonnegative counts, empty groups are permitted; for positive counts, reserve one unit per bin first. More general lower bounds can likewise be removed by shifting each variable. A remaining upper bound is not handled by the unrestricted formula: split into cases or subtract allocations violating that bound. The bins are distinguishable even when the units are not, so swapping two unequal bin totals generally changes the outcome. The method counts integer solutions; it does not apply to arbitrary real-valued allocations or distinct objects without changing the model.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Count nonnegative integer solutions to x+y+z=5.', r'Encode the allocation::::Five identical stars are split into three labeled groups by two bars.~Count available positions::5+2=7::The two bars occupy positions among seven total symbols, allowing adjacent bars.~Choose the bars::\binom72=21::Each arrangement determines exactly one nonnegative ordered triple.', 'Twenty-one solutions.', 'Summing by x gives 6+5+4+3+2+1=21 possible pairs (y,z).'),
E('application', 'Distribute twelve identical tokens among four labeled boxes, each receiving at least two. How many allocations exist?', r'Remove mandatory tokens::u_i=x_i-2\ge0::Reserve two tokens in each of the four boxes.~Compute the remaining total::u_1+u_2+u_3+u_4=12-8=4::Only four tokens remain freely allocatable.~Apply stars and bars::\binom{4+4-1}{4-1}=\binom73=35::Three bars separate four stars into four possibly empty groups.', 'Thirty-five allocations.', 'Adding two back to every bin gives a bijection between the counted nonnegative solutions and all permitted original allocations.'),
E('trap', 'Count nonnegative solutions to x+y+z=4 with x≤1. Why is unrestricted stars and bars insufficient?', r'Count all unrestricted solutions::\binom62=15::This count includes x values exceeding one.~Count the forbidden allocations::u=x-2\ge0,\quad u+y+z=2::Shifting x by two bijects all cases with x≥2 to a nonnegative three-bin allocation.~Subtract violations::15-\binom42=15-6=9::Remove every forbidden solution exactly once.', 'Nine solutions.', 'Splitting allowed cases gives five pairs when x=0 and four when x=1, also totaling nine.'))

P('0-recurrences-recurrences', 'Recurrence relations', r'''
A recurrence specifies how later values depend on earlier ones, while initial conditions choose a particular sequence from the possible solutions. Transforming a recurrence can simplify its structure: subtract a fixed point to remove a constant term, or classify combinatorial objects by their final symbol to derive a counting recurrence. For a constant-coefficient linear recurrence, characteristic roots describe the homogeneous solutions, but repeated roots require an extra polynomial factor in the index. Always check both the recurrence and every initial value after finding a formula. The range of valid indices matters because a second-order rule needs two starting values and should not be applied where earlier terms are undefined.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Solve aₙ=2aₙ₋₁+3 for n≥1 with a₀=1.', r'Remove the constant term::b_n=a_n+3\Rightarrow b_n=2b_{n-1}::Adding three shifts the recurrence to a geometric one.~Use the initial value::b_0=4\Rightarrow b_n=4\cdot2^n::The multiplier is two at every step.~Recover the sequence::a_n=4\cdot2^n-3::Undo the variable shift.', 'aₙ=4·2ⁿ−3.', 'At n=0 the formula gives one; substituting it into 2aₙ₋₁+3 reproduces 4·2ⁿ−3.'),
E('application', 'Let aₙ count binary strings of length n with no consecutive ones. Derive the recurrence and find a₅.', r'Partition by the ending::::An ending zero can follow any valid length n−1 string; an ending one must follow a final zero.~Count the two classes::a_n=a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}\quad(n\ge2)::Appending zero gives the first class; appending 01 to a valid shorter string gives the second.~Start and compute::a_0=1,\ a_1=2,\ a_2=3,\ a_3=5,\ a_4=8,\ a_5=13::The empty string counts once, supplying the correct starting value.', 'aₙ=aₙ₋₁+aₙ₋₂ with a₀=1,a₁=2; a₅=13.', 'Classifying length-five strings by whether their first symbol is zero or starts 10 gives the same 8+5 split.'),
E('trap', 'Solve aₙ=2aₙ₋₁−aₙ₋₂, a₀=0,a₁=1. Why is a constant multiple of 1ⁿ insufficient?', r'Find the repeated root::r^2-2r+1=(r-1)^2::The root one has multiplicity two.~Use the full solution form::a_n=(A+Bn)1^n::The factor n supplies the second independent solution needed for two initial conditions.~Fit and check::A=0,\ B=1,\quad2(n-1)-(n-2)=n::The resulting sequence satisfies the recurrence and both starts.', 'aₙ=n.', 'The first values 0,1,2,3 are not constant, so a lone A·1ⁿ cannot fit them.'))

P('0-recurrences-series', 'Generating functions', r'''
An ordinary generating function packages sequence values as coefficients, turning index shifts into algebraic multiplication by the variable. The generating function is usually manipulated as a formal power series, so convergence is unnecessary for coefficient identities; an analytic interpretation requires its own radius of convergence. Multiplying series convolves their coefficients, which provides a combinatorial interpretation of many rational forms. When translating a recurrence, subtract the initial terms missing from the shifted sum before solving. The numerator records those boundary conditions and is not optional. Verify a resulting expression by expanding its first few coefficients and comparing them with the recurrence, then use coefficient extraction for the desired index.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Find the ordinary generating function for aₙ=1 for every n≥0.', r'Write the series::A(x)=1+x+x^2+x^3+\cdots::Every coefficient is one.~Multiply by the denominator::(1-x)A(x)=1::All positive-degree coefficients cancel term by term.~Solve formally::A(x)=\frac1{1-x}::The constant-term-one denominator has a valid formal power-series inverse.', 'A(x)=1/(1−x).', 'Its geometric expansion has coefficient one at every degree; analytically that expansion converges for |x|<1.'),
E('application', 'Find the generating function for aₙ=n+1 using a convolution argument.', r'Multiply two unit sequences::\left(\sum_{i\ge0}x^i\right)\left(\sum_{j\ge0}x^j\right)::A degree-n term corresponds to nonnegative indices i+j=n.~Count the coefficient::[x^n]A(x)=n+1::The possible pairs are (0,n),(1,n−1),…,(n,0).~Simplify the product::A(x)=\frac1{(1-x)^2}::Each factor is the geometric generating function.', '1/(1−x)².', 'Differentiating x/(1−x) gives 1/(1−x)² and series coefficients n+1, an independent algebraic check.'),
E('trap', 'For Fibonacci starts f₀=0,f₁=1 and fₙ=fₙ₋₁+fₙ₋₂, is the generating function 1/(1−x−x²)?', r'Keep the starting terms::F(x)-x=xF(x)+x^2F(x)::Summing from n=2 leaves f₁x=x on the left and f₀=0 removes other boundary terms.~Solve the corrected equation::F(x)=\frac{x}{1-x-x^2}::The numerator preserves the initial zero coefficient.~Check the proposed alternative::[x^0]\frac1{1-x-x^2}=1\ne f_0::Its constant term already contradicts the specified sequence.', 'No; the correct function is x/(1−x−x²).', 'Expanding gives 0+x+x²+2x³+3x⁴+…, whereas the numerator-one expression starts 1+x+2x²+….'))

P('0-matrices-rank-matrix-basics', 'Matrices', r'''
A matrix represents an indexed array or a linear map, and dimensions determine which operations have meaning. Multiplication composes maps by taking row-column dot products; its order records which map acts first. Transposition swaps indices and reverses multiplication order in a product transpose. Equal dimensions permit addition but do not guarantee commutativity of multiplication. Before computing entries, write the dimensions of every operand and predicted output. This often resolves a question without arithmetic. Small explicit products can reveal noncommutativity or a claimed identity’s failure. Unless a question states otherwise, use ordinary real matrix arithmetic; Boolean or modular matrix operations follow different scalar rules.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Transpose A=[[1,0,2],[−1,3,4]] and state its dimensions.', r'Read the input shape::A\in\mathbb R^{2\times3}::There are two rows and three columns.~Swap row and column indices::(A^{\mathsf T})_{ij}=A_{ji}::Each original column becomes a row.~Write the output::A^{\mathsf T}=\begin{bmatrix}1&-1\\0&3\\2&4\end{bmatrix}::The transpose has three rows and two columns.', 'Aᵀ=[[1,−1],[0,3],[2,4]], with shape 3×2.', 'Transposing the result again restores each original row and the 2×3 shape.'),
E('application', 'For A=[[1,1],[0,1]] and B=[[1,0],[1,1]], compute AB and BA to test commutativity.', r'Compute the first product::AB=\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\1&1\end{bmatrix}::Use each A row against each B column.~Reverse the order::BA=\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\1&2\end{bmatrix}::Now the row-column combinations change.~Compare entries::(AB)_{11}=2\ne1=(BA)_{11}::A single unequal entry disproves equality of the matrices.', 'AB≠BA; the products are as displayed.', 'Both determinants equal det(A)det(B)=1, showing equal determinants alone do not make the matrices equal.'),
E('trap', 'A is 2×3 and B is 2×2. Which of AB and BA is defined?', r'Test AB inner dimensions::(2\times3)(2\times2)::Three does not equal two, so AB is undefined.~Test BA inner dimensions::(2\times2)(2\times3)::The shared dimension two matches, so BA is defined.~Predict the valid output::BA\in\mathbb R^{2\times3}::The outside dimensions determine the result shape.', 'Only BA is defined, with shape 2×3.', 'As maps, A sends three-dimensional inputs to two dimensions; B can act after A, but A cannot act after B on its two-dimensional output.'))

P('0-matrices-rank-determinants', 'Determinants', r'''
The determinant measures a square linear map’s signed volume scaling and detects invertibility. Row operations let you compute it efficiently, but their effects must be tracked: adding a multiple of another row preserves it, swapping rows negates it, and multiplying a row by a scalar multiplies it by that scalar. For triangular matrices, the diagonal product gives the determinant after those corrections. Multiplication has a clean identity det(AB)=det(A)det(B); addition has no analogous general identity. A nonzero determinant implies full rank, whereas zero signals a nontrivial dependence among rows or columns. These statements concern square matrices and ordinary field arithmetic; determinants are not defined for a general rectangular matrix.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Compute det([[3,2],[1,4]]).', r'Use the two-by-two formula::\det\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{bmatrix}=ad-bc::The opposite diagonal products have opposite signs.~Compute each product::3\cdot4=12,\quad2\cdot1=2::Keep their order consistent.~Subtract::12-2=10::The determinant is nonzero, so the matrix is invertible.', 'Ten.', 'Row elimination R₂←R₂−R₁/3 gives diagonal entries 3 and 10/3, whose product is ten.'),
E('application', 'Matrix A has determinant 5. Form B by scaling its first row by 3 and then swapping its first two rows. Find det(B).', r'Track row scaling::\det(A_1)=3\det(A)=15::Only one row is multiplied, so the factor is three.~Track the row exchange::\det(B)=-\det(A_1)::One swap reverses the determinant sign.~Combine effects::\det(B)=-15::The operations multiply the original determinant by −3.', '−15.', 'Undoing the swap and dividing the scaled row by three multiplies −15 by −1/3, returning five.'),
E('trap', 'Is det(A+B)=det(A)+det(B)? Test A=B=I₂.', r'Evaluate individual determinants::\det(I_2)=1::Each identity matrix has determinant one.~Evaluate the sum::A+B=2I_2,\quad\det(2I_2)=2\cdot2=4::Both diagonal directions are scaled by two.~Compare with the claimed right side::1+1=2\ne4::The example disproves additivity.', 'No; determinants are not generally additive.', 'The scaling law det(cA)=cⁿdet(A) for an n×n matrix predicts four here, not two.'))

P('0-matrices-rank-systems', 'System of linear equations', r'''
A linear system asks whether the constant vector lies in the span of the coefficient columns and, if it does, how many coefficient choices represent it. Row operations on the augmented matrix preserve the solution set when applied to constants as well as coefficients. A contradiction row proves inconsistency; otherwise each free variable contributes a parameter. Parameters inside equations require a case split whenever a pivot coefficient could vanish. More equations than unknowns do not automatically mean inconsistency, because some equations may duplicate others. Verify proposed solutions in every original equation, not only the simplified system, to catch arithmetic or an omitted constraint.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Solve 2x+y=5 and x−y=1.', r'Add the equations::3x=6::The y terms cancel without changing the solution set.~Solve the first variable::x=2::Divide the reduced equation by three.~Recover the second variable::2-y=1\Rightarrow y=1::Substitute into the second original equation.', 'x=2,y=1.', 'The first equation gives 2·2+1=5 and the second gives 2−1=1.'),
E('application', 'Classify solutions of x+y=1 and x+ay=b for all real a,b.', r'Eliminate x::(a-1)y=b-1::Subtract the first equation from the second.~Handle a nonzero pivot::a\ne1\Rightarrow y=\frac{b-1}{a-1},\quad x=\frac{a-b}{a-1}::A nonzero coefficient yields one unique solution.~Handle the zero pivot::a=1\Rightarrow0=b-1::If b=1 the equations coincide and solutions are infinite; otherwise they contradict.', 'Unique for a≠1; infinitely many for a=b=1; none for a=1,b≠1.', 'Substituting the unique formulas yields x+y=1 and x+ay=b; the special cases avoid division by zero.'),
E('trap', 'Do three equations in two unknowns necessarily have no solution? Examine 2x+y=3,4x+2y=6,6x+3y=9.', r'Compare the second equation::::It is twice the first, so it adds no independent restriction.~Compare the third equation::::It is three times the first, again adding no independent restriction.~Parameterize the remaining condition::x=t,\quad y=3-2t\quad(t\in\mathbb R)::One free variable remains after the single independent equation.', 'No; this system has infinitely many solutions.', 'Every parameter value satisfies all three equations because the last two are exact multiples of the first.'))

P('0-matrices-rank-independence-rank', 'Rank', r'''
Rank counts independent directions in a matrix’s image, equivalently the number of pivot positions after legal elimination. It is bounded by both the row count and column count and is unchanged by invertible row operations. A zero appearing on the original diagonal does not imply a missing pivot, because a row exchange may expose it elsewhere. For a linear map with n inputs, rank-nullity separates n into independent output directions and kernel freedom. A rank computation can be checked through nonzero minors and explicit dependence: a nonzero r-by-r minor gives a lower bound, while dependence or elimination gives the upper bound. Use coefficient rank rather than augmented rank when discussing the map itself.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Find the rank of [[1,2],[2,4]].', r'Identify row dependence::R_2=2R_1::The second row adds no new direction.~Eliminate the dependent row::R_2\leftarrow R_2-2R_1=(0,0)::The row-equivalent matrix has just one nonzero row.~Count the pivot::\operatorname{rank}(A)=1::The first row is nonzero, supplying exactly one independent direction.', 'One.', 'The determinant is zero but an entry is nonzero, so rank is below two and at least one.'),
E('application', 'A real matrix A is 3×4 with rank 2. Find the dimension of {x∈ℝ⁴:Ax=0} and of the image.', r'Use the input dimension::\dim(\mathbb R^4)=4::Nullity concerns the four-coordinate input space.~Apply rank-nullity::\dim\ker A=4-2=2::Two input degrees of freedom map to zero.~Read the image dimension::\dim\operatorname{im}A=2::Rank is defined as the dimension of the image.', 'Kernel dimension two; image dimension two.', 'The dimensions sum to the four input directions, while the image dimension remains within the three-dimensional codomain.'),
E('trap', 'Does A=[[0,1],[1,0]] have rank zero because its original diagonal entries are zero?', r'Exchange the rows::R_1\leftrightarrow R_2::The swap is invertible and preserves rank.~Inspect the resulting matrix::\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{bmatrix}::It has two pivots despite the original zero diagonal.~Count independent directions::\operatorname{rank}(A)=2::Both rows and columns are independent.', 'No; its rank is two.', 'det(A)=−1 is nonzero, independently proving full rank.'))

P('0-matrices-rank-independence-rank', 'Linear independence', r'''
Vectors are linearly independent when the only linear combination giving zero uses all-zero coefficients. This definition explains why a single nonzero vector is independent but several individually nonzero vectors may still depend on one another. To disprove independence, provide one explicit nontrivial zero combination; to prove it, solve the coefficient equations and show every coefficient vanishes. For n vectors in an n-dimensional space, a nonzero determinant supplies the same proof compactly. More vectors than the ambient dimension must be dependent. Independence concerns the whole collection, not whether just one selected pair has distinct entries or whether none of the vectors equals zero.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Are (1,0),(0,1),(1,1) linearly independent in ℝ²?', r'Express the third vector::(1,1)=(1,0)+(0,1)::It is already in the span of the first two.~Rearrange to zero::-(1,0)-(0,1)+(1,1)=(0,0)::The coefficients form a zero combination.~Check nontriviality::(-1,-1,1)\ne(0,0,0)::The definition therefore declares the collection dependent.', 'Dependent.', 'Three vectors in a two-dimensional space cannot be independent, confirming the explicit dependence.'),
E('application', 'Determine independence of v₁=(1,1,0),v₂=(1,0,1),v₃=(0,1,1).', r'Form coefficient equations::c_1+c_2=0,\quad c_1+c_3=0,\quad c_2+c_3=0::These are the coordinates of c₁v₁+c₂v₂+c₃v₃=0.~Eliminate coefficients::c_2=-c_1,\quad c_3=-c_1\Rightarrow-2c_1=0::The last equation forces c₁ to vanish.~Recover the others::c_1=c_2=c_3=0::Only the trivial zero combination exists.', 'Independent.', 'The matrix with these vectors as columns has determinant −2, a nonzero independent check.'),
E('trap', 'Are the nonzero vectors (1,2) and (2,4) independent because neither is zero?', r'Compare the vectors::(2,4)=2(1,2)::The second is a scalar multiple of the first.~Build a zero combination::2(1,2)-(2,4)=(0,0)::Both coefficients are nonzero.~Apply the definition::::One nontrivial zero combination disproves independence, irrespective of individual nonzero entries.', 'No; they are dependent.', 'The two-by-two column determinant is 1·4−2·2=0, and both vectors lie on the same line.'))

P('0-matrices-rank-gaussian', 'Gaussian elimination', r'''
Gaussian elimination reduces a system through invertible row operations, preserving its entire solution set. Work with the augmented matrix so each operation also changes its constant. A zero candidate pivot can often be repaired by swapping rows rather than dividing by zero. After clearing entries below successive pivots, back-substitute from the last nonzero equation upward. If elimination yields a zero coefficient row with a nonzero constant, stop: no solution exists. Exact fractions are preferable to premature rounding in hand calculations. Verify the final answer in the original equations, because an arithmetic mistake in elimination may otherwise remain hidden in every later step.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Use elimination to solve x+2y=5 and 3x+4y=11.', r'Write the augmented matrix::\begin{bmatrix}1&2&5\\3&4&11\end{bmatrix}::The last column stores constants.~Eliminate below the first pivot::R_2\leftarrow R_2-3R_1=[0,-2,-4]::Apply the subtraction to all three entries.~Back substitute::-2y=-4\Rightarrow y=2,\quad x+4=5\Rightarrow x=1::Solve the final row first, then the first.', 'x=1,y=2.', 'The original left sides are 1+4=5 and 3+8=11.'),
E('application', 'Solve y+z=3, x+y=3, 2x+y+z=7 using a row swap to obtain the first pivot.', r'Swap the first two rows::\begin{bmatrix}1&1&0&3\\0&1&1&3\\2&1&1&7\end{bmatrix}::The new first row supplies a nonzero x pivot.~Eliminate the third row::R_3\leftarrow R_3-2R_1=[0,-1,1,1],\quad R_3\leftarrow R_3+R_2=[0,0,2,4]::Successive pivots remove x and y.~Back substitute::z=2,\quad y=1,\quad x=2::The triangular system yields one value per variable.', 'x=2,y=1,z=2.', 'The original equations give 1+2=3,2+1=3, and 4+1+2=7.'),
E('trap', 'Eliminate x from x+y=2 and 2x+2y=5. Why must the constant column be changed too?', r'Apply the full row operation::R_2\leftarrow R_2-2R_1::Every coefficient and the constant must undergo the same subtraction.~Inspect the resulting row::[0,0\mid1]::The left side cancels, but five minus four leaves one.~Interpret the equation::0=1::This contradiction proves the original system is inconsistent.', 'There is no solution.', 'Doubling the first original equation requires 2x+2y=4, directly contradicting the stated value five.'))

P('0-matrices-rank-gaussian', 'Row echelon form', r'''
Row echelon form organizes independent equations rather than specifying a unique matrix. Nonzero rows precede zero rows, each leading entry lies to the right of the preceding row’s leading entry, and entries below a pivot vanish. Pivot values need not equal one, and entries above pivots need not vanish; those additional conditions belong to reduced row echelon form. Reading pivot and free columns identifies which variables are determined and which can parameterize the solution set. A triangular-looking matrix can still fail the definition if a zero row interrupts nonzero rows. Check the structural conditions in order, then translate each augmented row back into an equation before solving.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Is [[1,2,0],[0,0,3],[0,0,0]] in row echelon form? Identify pivot columns.', r'Check zero-row placement::::The only zero row is last, as required.~Locate the leading entries::::The first row leads in column one and the second in column three.~Check pivot ordering::::Three lies to the right of one, and all entries below those pivots are zero.', 'Yes; pivot columns are one and three, with column two free in a homogeneous system.', 'The pivot three need not be normalized to one for ordinary echelon form.'),
E('application', 'Read all solutions from the augmented reduced echelon matrix [[1,0,2,5],[0,1,−1,1],[0,0,0,0]].', r'Translate the rows::x+2z=5,\quad y-z=1::The last column is the constant column.~Choose the free variable::z=t\quad(t\in\mathbb R)::Column three lacks a pivot and may vary freely.~Recover pivot variables::x=5-2t,\quad y=1+t::The two pivot equations determine their variables from t.', '(x,y,z)=(5−2t,1+t,t), for every real t.', 'Substituting this parameterization into both displayed equations yields five and one for all t.'),
E('trap', 'Is [[0,1,2],[0,0,0],[0,0,3]] in echelon form merely because entries below the diagonal are zero?', r'Find the zero row::::The second row is entirely zero.~Find a later nonzero row::::The third row has leading entry three, so a nonzero row follows a zero row.~Apply the actual condition::::Zero rows must appear below every nonzero row; exchange rows two and three to repair the order.', 'No; its zero-row placement violates echelon form.', 'After the exchange the pivots lie in columns two and three with increasing positions, confirming the precise structural repair.'))

P('0-eigen-lu-eigen', 'Eigenvalues', r'''
An eigenvalue identifies a scalar stretch possible along a preserved direction of a square linear map. The characteristic equation finds candidate scalars by asking when A−λI becomes singular. Trace and determinant give independent checks on the sum and product of eigenvalues, counting algebraic multiplicity. Triangular matrices expose eigenvalues on the diagonal even when their off-diagonal entries are nonzero. A repeated characteristic root does not guarantee enough independent eigenvectors for diagonalization. Distinguish algebraic multiplicity, which comes from the polynomial, from geometric multiplicity, which comes from the nullspace. State the field: a real matrix may have complex eigenvalues, so absence of real roots is not absence of eigenvalues over complex numbers.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Find eigenvalues of A=[[3,2],[0,1]].', r'Form the characteristic determinant::\det(A-\lambda I)=(3-\lambda)(1-\lambda)::Triangular structure makes the determinant a diagonal product.~Solve for singularity::\lambda=3\text{ or }1::Each root makes A−λI singular.~Check the invariants::3+1=4=\operatorname{tr}A,\quad3\cdot1=3=\det A::Both aggregate checks agree.', 'Eigenvalues three and one.', 'The off-diagonal two changes eigendirections but does not change a triangular matrix’s diagonal eigenvalues.'),
E('application', 'Find the eigenvalues of [[2,2],[2,2]] and determine whether it is invertible.', r'Compute the polynomial::(2-\lambda)^2-4=\lambda(\lambda-4)::Expand the determinant of A−λI.~Find both roots::\lambda=0,4::The zero eigenvalue corresponds to a nonzero kernel direction.~Infer invertibility::\det A=0\cdot4=0::The zero eigenvalue makes the matrix singular.', 'Eigenvalues zero and four; not invertible.', 'A(1,−1)ᵀ=0 and A(1,1)ᵀ=4(1,1)ᵀ directly verify both eigenvalues.'),
E('trap', 'A=[[2,1],[0,2]] has repeated eigenvalue two. Must it have two independent eigenvectors?', r'Read algebraic multiplicity::\det(A-\lambda I)=(2-\lambda)^2::The characteristic root is repeated twice.~Solve its eigenvector condition::(A-2I)v=0\Rightarrow v_2=0::The eigenspace consists only of vectors (t,0).~Count independent directions::::This space is one-dimensional, so it cannot supply two independent eigenvectors.', 'No; the repeated root has geometric multiplicity one.', 'The matrix is not 2I despite having only eigenvalue two; its nonzero upper-right entry creates the missing direction.'))

P('0-eigen-lu-eigen', 'Eigenvectors', r'''
An eigenvector must be nonzero and satisfy the vector equation Av=λv, not merely resemble a column of the matrix. Once an eigenvalue is known, solve the homogeneous system (A−λI)v=0 and retain every nonzero vector in that nullspace. Scalar multiples describe the same direction, while a multidimensional eigenspace contains several independent directions. The zero vector belongs to the nullspace but is excluded from eigenvector terminology because it supplies no direction and would satisfy the equation for every scalar. Verify a proposed pair through direct multiplication rather than only a characteristic root. These procedures assume square matrices; a general rectangular map does not have ordinary same-space eigenvectors.
''',
E('fundamental', 'For A=[[2,1],[1,2]], test v=(1,−1)ᵀ and identify its eigenvalue.', r'Multiply the vector::Av=\begin{bmatrix}2-1\\1-2\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\end{bmatrix}::Compute both row-column dot products.~Compare with the original::Av=1v::The output is exactly a scalar multiple with scalar one.~Check nonzero membership::v\ne0::The vector satisfies the definition rather than the trivial zero equation.', 'v is an eigenvector for eigenvalue one.', 'The characteristic polynomial (2−λ)²−1 has root one; (1,1) provides the distinct eigenvalue-three direction.'),
E('application', 'For A=diag(3,3,1), describe all eigenvectors for eigenvalue three and give an eigenspace basis.', r'Subtract the eigenvalue::A-3I=\operatorname{diag}(0,0,-2)::Only the third coordinate is constrained.~Solve the kernel::v=(s,t,0),\quad(s,t)\ne(0,0)::The first two coordinates are free, with the zero vector excluded.~Choose independent generators::(1,0,0),\quad(0,1,0)::These span the two-dimensional eigenspace.', 'All nonzero (s,t,0); a basis is the first two coordinate vectors.', 'Multiplying any such vector by A gives (3s,3t,0)=3v, verifying the entire family.'),
E('trap', 'Because A0=λ0 for every λ, should zero be counted as an eigenvector for every scalar?', r'Evaluate the equation::A0=0=\lambda0::The equality indeed holds trivially.~Apply the nonzero requirement::::The eigenvector definition explicitly excludes zero because it has no direction.~Recover the real test::\ker(A-\lambda I)\ne\{0\}::An eigenvalue needs a nontrivial nullspace, not merely the unavoidable zero solution.', 'No; zero is never an eigenvector.', 'For I₂, zero satisfies the equation at λ=7, yet I₂−7I₂=−6I₂ is invertible and seven is not an eigenvalue.'))

P('0-eigen-lu-lu', 'LU decomposition', r'''
LU decomposition records elimination in reusable triangular factors. In the standard convention, L has unit diagonal and contains elimination multipliers; U contains the resulting upper-triangular rows. Solving Ax=b then separates into Ly=b by forward substitution and Ux=y by backward substitution, avoiding repeated elimination for new right-hand sides. A zero pivot can obstruct an unpivoted factorization even when A is invertible, so row pivoting introduces a permutation P and the identity PA=LU. Check a factorization by multiplying L and U, not merely observing their shapes. Keep any row permutation consistent with b when solving. The factorization is useful precisely because each triangular system can be solved in a fixed order.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Factor A=[[1,2],[3,8]] as LU with unit diagonal in L.', r'Compute the multiplier::m_{21}=3/1=3::Use row one to clear the first entry of row two.~Form U::R_2\leftarrow R_2-3R_1=(0,2),\quad U=\begin{bmatrix}1&2\\0&2\end{bmatrix}::The resulting matrix is upper triangular.~Record L::L=\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\3&1\end{bmatrix}::The multiplier enters L below its unit diagonal.', 'L=[[1,0],[3,1]], U=[[1,2],[0,2]].', 'Multiplication gives second row (3,6+2)=(3,8), reconstructing A exactly.'),
E('application', 'Given L=[[1,0,0],[2,1,0],[1,−1,1]], U=[[2,1,0],[0,3,1],[0,0,4]], solve LUx=(4,17,7)ᵀ.', r'Forward substitute::y_1=4,\quad2y_1+y_2=17\Rightarrow y_2=9,\quad y_1-y_2+y_3=7\Rightarrow y_3=12::Lower-triangular rows determine y in order.~Start backward substitution::4x_3=12\Rightarrow x_3=3,\quad3x_2+x_3=9\Rightarrow x_2=2::Upper-triangular rows are solved from the bottom.~Finish the first row::2x_1+x_2=4\Rightarrow x_1=1::The remaining variable follows from the known x₂.', 'x=(1,2,3)ᵀ.', 'LU=[[2,1,0],[4,5,1],[2,−2,3]]; its product with x gives (4,17,7)ᵀ.'),
E('trap', 'Can A=[[0,1],[1,0]] have an unpivoted LU with unit-diagonal lower L, despite being invertible?', r'Write the possible factors::L=\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\l&1\end{bmatrix},\quad U=\begin{bmatrix}u&v\\0&w\end{bmatrix}::These are the required triangular shapes.~Compare the first column::u=0,\quad lu=1::The top-left entry forces u=0, contradicting the lower-left equation.~Introduce a permutation::::Swapping A’s rows gives I₂, which factors as I₂I₂, so PA=LU works.', 'No unpivoted factorization under this convention; pivoting fixes it.', 'det(A)=−1 verifies invertibility, proving that invertibility alone does not guarantee a nonzero first unpivoted pivot.'))

P('0-limits-continuity-limits', 'Limits', r'''
A limit describes values near a point, excluding the point itself when necessary. This is why canceling a factor for nearby unequal inputs can establish a limit even when the original formula is undefined at the target. At infinity, divide by the dominant power to compare growth rates. At a finite junction, compare left and right approaches; matching a few sampled values or knowing the expression is bounded is insufficient. Use transformations valid on a punctured neighborhood and state any sign assumptions introduced by roots or absolute values. After simplifying, invoke continuity only for the transformed expression where it is actually defined. A two-sided limit exists only when both one-sided limits exist and agree.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Evaluate limₓ→₃ (x²−9)/(x−3).', r'Factor the numerator::x^2-9=(x-3)(x+3)::The removable factor explains the zero-over-zero form.~Cancel near the point::\frac{x^2-9}{x-3}=x+3\quad(x\ne3)::Cancellation is valid for all nearby inputs used by the limit.~Take the continuous limit::\lim_{x\to3}(x+3)=6::The simplified polynomial is continuous at three.', 'Six.', 'Values x=3−h and x=3+h become 6−h and 6+h, both approaching six.'),
E('application', 'Find limₙ→∞ (3n²+n)/(2n²−5).', r'Divide by the dominant power::\frac{3n^2+n}{2n^2-5}=\frac{3+1/n}{2-5/n^2}::This exposes the lower-order terms.~Take the component limits::1/n\to0,\quad5/n^2\to0::Both corrections vanish as n grows.~Use the quotient limit::\frac{3+0}{2-0}=\frac32::The limiting denominator is nonzero.', 'Three halves.', 'Subtracting 3/2 gives (2n+15)/(4n²−10), which tends to zero directly.'),
E('trap', 'Does x/|x| have a two-sided limit at zero because it is bounded?', r'Approach from the right::x>0\Rightarrow x/|x|=1::The right-hand limit is one.~Approach from the left::x<0\Rightarrow x/|x|=-1::The left-hand limit is minus one.~Compare the limits::1\ne-1::Boundedness cannot replace agreement of the two approaches.', 'No two-sided limit exists.', 'Sequences xₙ=1/n and yₙ=−1/n converge to zero but give different constant output sequences.'))

P('0-limits-continuity-continuity', 'Continuity', r'''
Continuity links a function’s assigned value to its nearby behavior. At a point, the value must exist, the two-sided limit must exist, and they must coincide; at an endpoint, use the applicable one-sided version. A removable discontinuity can be repaired by assigning the limiting value, whereas a jump with unequal one-sided limits cannot be repaired by changing one isolated value. For piecewise formulas, calculate each side from the branch that actually applies there. Parameters governing the value at a junction may differ from parameters governing slope, so solving a continuity condition alone does not establish differentiability. Check all three requirements rather than treating existence of a limit as a complete continuity proof.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Let f(x)=(x²−1)/(x−1) for x≠1 and f(1)=c. Choose c for continuity at one.', r'Simplify nearby inputs::f(x)=x+1\quad(x\ne1)::Factoring removes the undefined denominator near the point.~Find the limiting value::\lim_{x\to1}f(x)=2::Both approaches use the same simplified expression.~Match the assigned value::c=f(1)=2::Continuity requires equality between the value and limit.', 'c=2.', 'With this choice the function equals x+1 everywhere; any other c leaves a single removable discontinuity.'),
E('application', 'For f(x)=ax+b when x<0 and f(x)=x²+1 when x≥0, find the continuity condition at zero.', r'Compute the left limit::\lim_{x\to0^-}(ax+b)=b::The slope coefficient does not contribute at the junction.~Compute the right limit and value::\lim_{x\to0^+}(x^2+1)=f(0)=1::The right branch includes zero.~Equate the required values::b=1::Any real a is allowed by continuity alone.', 'b=1, with arbitrary real a.', 'Both side limits then equal one; differentiability would impose an additional slope condition a=0.'),
E('trap', 'Let f(0)=1 and f(x)=0 for x≠0. Is f continuous at zero because its two-sided limit exists?', r'Find the neighboring limit::\lim_{x\to0}f(x)=0::Every nonzero nearby input has output zero.~Read the actual value::f(0)=1::The exceptional point has a different assigned value.~Compare::0\ne1::Existence of the limit does not satisfy the equality requirement.', 'No; f is discontinuous at zero.', 'For ε=1/2, every nonzero x arbitrarily close to zero has |f(x)−f(0)|=1, violating continuity.'))

P('0-limits-continuity-differentiability-corner', 'Differentiability', r'''
Differentiability is a local linear-approximation condition, and the difference quotient tests the slope of that approximation. At a piecewise junction, continuity is necessary but the one-sided slopes must also agree. A corner may disappear when multiplied by a vanishing factor, so do not infer differentiability solely from an absolute-value symbol. Derivative existence also does not imply that the derivative function is continuous: oscillations can survive differentiation even when the original quotient converges. Use the original function value in the numerator and choose increments approaching from both sides. These examples use real finite derivatives; an infinite slope is not counted as differentiable under that convention.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Is f(x)=x|x| differentiable at zero? Find f′(0).', r'Use the value at zero::f(0)=0::The difference quotient uses this actual value.~Simplify the quotient::\frac{f(h)-f(0)}h=|h|\quad(h\ne0)::The factor h cancels for every permitted increment.~Take both approaches::\lim_{h\to0}|h|=0::The same finite limit holds from either side.', 'Yes; f′(0)=0.', 'Away from zero, the derivative is 2|x|, whose limit at zero also equals zero; the absolute-value factor alone does not force a corner here.'),
E('application', 'Choose a,b so that f(x)=x² for x≤1 and f(x)=ax+b for x>1 is differentiable at one.', r'Impose continuity::a+b=1::Both branches must approach the actual value f(1)=1.~Match the one-sided derivatives::a=2::The left slope of x² at one is two and the right linear slope is a.~Solve both conditions::a=2,\quad b=-1::Slope agreement alone would not have imposed the correct value.', 'a=2,b=−1.', 'The right line 2x−1 is precisely the tangent line to x² at (1,1), independently explaining the match.'),
E('trap', 'Let f(0)=0 and f(x)=x²sin(1/x) otherwise. Does differentiability at zero guarantee continuity of f′ there?', r'Compute the defining derivative::f^{\prime}(0)=\lim_{h\to0}h\sin(1/h)=0::Its absolute value is bounded by |h|.~Differentiate away from zero::f^{\prime}(x)=2x\sin(1/x)-\cos(1/x)::The chain rule creates an oscillating term.~Compare approaching sequences::x_n=\frac1{2\pi n},\quad y_n=\frac1{(2n+1)\pi}::The derivatives are respectively −1 and +1, so f′ has no limit at zero.', 'No; this function is differentiable at zero but f′ is discontinuous there.', 'Both sequences approach zero, while their distinct derivative limits contradict the continuity value f′(0)=0.'))

P('0-limits-continuity-derivative', 'Differentiation', r'''
Derivative rules combine local changes according to expression structure. A sum allows termwise differentiation, a composition requires the inner derivative, and a quotient combines numerator and denominator changes with a squared denominator. Identify that structure before simplifying or substituting a numerical point. Domain restrictions remain relevant after differentiation: a formula produced by cancellation is not automatically valid at points excluded from the original function. Compare a complicated calculation with an algebraic rewrite when one is available. The derivative gives a slope or rate, so its units are output units divided by input units. These rules require differentiable factors and a nonzero denominator on the neighborhood under consideration.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Differentiate f(x)=3x²−4x+7.', r'Differentiate the quadratic::(3x^2)^{\prime}=6x::The power rule multiplies by two and decreases the exponent.~Differentiate the remaining terms::(-4x)^{\prime}=-4,\quad7^{\prime}=0::A constant has no local change.~Add the derivatives::f^{\prime}(x)=6x-4::Linearity permits termwise addition.', '6x−4.', 'The difference quotient expands to 6x+3h−4 and tends to 6x−4 as h→0.'),
E('application', 'For f(x)=exp(3x²+1), find the slope at x=1.', r'Identify the composition::u=3x^2+1,\quad f=e^u::The exponential acts on a varying inner expression.~Apply the chain rule::f^{\prime}(x)=e^{3x^2+1}\cdot6x::The outer derivative must be multiplied by the inner derivative.~Evaluate at the point::f^{\prime}(1)=6e^4::Substitute only after obtaining the symbolic derivative.', '6e⁴.', 'At x=1 the inner expression is four and its slope is six, so outer rate e⁴ times inner rate six reproduces the answer.'),
E('trap', 'Is the derivative of x/(1+x) equal to 1/1? Compute it for x≠−1.', r'Apply the quotient rule::f^{\prime}(x)=\frac{1(1+x)-x(1)}{(1+x)^2}::Differentiating numerator and denominator separately as a ratio is invalid.~Simplify the numerator::1+x-x=1::Both contributions are required for this cancellation.~Keep the domain::f^{\prime}(x)=\frac1{(1+x)^2},\quad x\ne-1::The original denominator excludes minus one.', '1/(1+x)².', 'Rewriting f=1−1/(1+x) and differentiating gives the same expression; at x=1 the derivative is 1/4, not one.'))

P('0-extrema-mvt-extrema', 'Maxima and minima', r'''
An extremum compares a function value with neighboring or all permitted values, so the domain determines which candidates matter. Interior differentiable extrema must have zero derivative, but a stationary point may fail to be an extremum. On a closed interval, evaluate endpoints as well as interior critical points, including points where derivatives do not exist. A derivative sign change gives local classification; direct comparison or a completed square can give global classification. Optimization word problems first require translating constraints into a one-variable function and a valid domain. The second derivative is useful when nonzero at a stationary point but inconclusive when zero; the function’s values or first-derivative signs then resolve the question.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Find the global minimum of f(x)=x²−4x+3 on ℝ.', r'Locate the stationary point::f^{\prime}(x)=2x-4=0\Rightarrow x=2::The derivative identifies the only interior candidate.~Complete the square::f(x)=(x-2)^2-1::The square is nonnegative for every real input.~Read the bound::f(x)\ge-1,\quad f(2)=-1::The candidate attains a global lower bound.', 'Minimum −1 at x=2.', 'f′ changes from negative to positive at two, independently confirming a minimum.'),
E('application', 'A rectangle has perimeter 20. Maximize its area allowing nonnegative side lengths.', r'Use the constraint::y=10-x,\quad0\le x\le10::The perimeter fixes the second side and the feasible interval.~Differentiate the area::A(x)=10x-x^2,\quad A^{\prime}(x)=10-2x::The stationary candidate is x=5.~Compare all candidates::A(5)=25,\quad A(0)=A(10)=0::The endpoints and interior candidate establish the global maximum.', 'Maximum area 25, attained by a 5×5 square.', 'A=25−(x−5)² supplies an independent global bound across the feasible interval.'),
E('trap', 'Does f(x)=x on [0,1] lack extrema because f′(x)=1 never vanishes?', r'Inspect monotonicity::f^{\prime}(x)=1>0::The function increases throughout the interval.~Check the endpoints::f(0)=0,\quad f(1)=1::Closed-domain endpoints are eligible extremum locations.~Compare all values::0\le f(x)\le1::The endpoint values attain the minimum and maximum despite no stationary point.', 'Minimum zero at x=0 and maximum one at x=1.', 'The zero-derivative necessity applies to interior differentiable extrema, not to boundary extrema.'))

P('0-extrema-mvt-mvt', 'Mean value theorem', r'''
The mean value theorem connects an average slope over an interval to an instantaneous slope inside it. Its hypotheses are continuity on the entire closed interval and differentiability at every interior point. Endpoints need not have derivatives, but their values must match the required one-sided continuity. Once the hypotheses hold, equate the derivative with the secant slope and find an interior witness. Bounds on derivatives then become bounds on total change, which is often more useful than finding the witness explicitly. Failing a hypothesis removes the guarantee; it does not automatically prove the conclusion false in every example. Check the conditions before using the theorem in an inequality or existence argument.
''',
E('fundamental', 'For f(x)=x² on [1,3], find a point c guaranteed by the mean value theorem.', r'Check the hypotheses::::A polynomial is continuous on the closed interval and differentiable on its interior.~Compute the secant slope::\frac{9-1}{3-1}=4::Use the actual endpoint values.~Match the derivative::2c=4\Rightarrow c=2\in(1,3)::The interior tangent slope equals the average slope.', 'c=2.', 'The tangent slope at two is four; the endpoint secant independently has rise eight over run two.'),
E('application', 'Use the mean value theorem to prove |sin b−sin a|≤|b−a| for all real a,b.', r'Handle equal endpoints::::If a=b, both sides are zero; otherwise reorder endpoints if necessary.~Apply the theorem::\sin b-\sin a=\cos c\,(b-a)::Sine meets the hypotheses on every finite real interval.~Bound the derivative::|\cos c|\le1\Rightarrow|\sin b-\sin a|\le|b-a|::Taking absolute values yields the inequality.', 'The inequality holds for all real a,b.', 'The derivative of sine is bounded in magnitude by one, so its total change cannot exceed the interval length.'),
E('trap', 'Let f(x)=0 for 0≤x<1 and f(1)=1. Can MVT on [0,1] guarantee an interior point with derivative one?', r'Compute the average slope::\frac{f(1)-f(0)}{1-0}=1::The endpoint jump makes the secant slope one.~Inspect interior derivatives::f^{\prime}(x)=0\quad(0<x<1)::Every interior neighborhood has the constant-zero branch.~Check the failed hypothesis::\lim_{x\to1^-}f(x)=0\ne f(1)::Continuity on the closed interval fails at the endpoint.', 'No; no such interior point exists, and MVT does not apply.', 'Differentiability throughout the open interval alone is insufficient; the endpoint continuity condition is essential.'))

P('0-extrema-mvt-differentiate-product', 'Differentiation', r'''
In optimization and rate questions, differentiation is a tool for extracting useful local information from a formula. Simplify the derivative into factors when seeking stationary inputs, but do not divide by a factor that might be zero and lose a candidate. Products, quotients and compositions need their own rules even if the final derivative later simplifies. Find the symbolic derivative before substituting a point, and keep the original function’s domain visible. A stationary-input calculation is distinct from classifying an extremum; classification requires an additional argument about signs or values. An independent algebraic rewrite, or substitution into the derivative definition at one point, can detect a dropped term or chain-rule factor.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Find the tangent slope of f(x)=x²/(x+1) at x=1.', r'Apply the quotient rule::f^{\prime}(x)=\frac{2x(x+1)-x^2}{(x+1)^2}::Both numerator and denominator change with x.~Simplify::f^{\prime}(x)=\frac{x(x+2)}{(x+1)^2}::Collecting terms makes point evaluation easy.~Substitute the point::f^{\prime}(1)=\frac{1\cdot3}{4}=\frac34::The point is inside the domain x≠−1.', 'Three quarters.', 'Polynomial division gives f=x−1+1/(x+1); its derivative at one is 1−1/4=3/4.'),
E('application', 'Find all stationary inputs of g(x)=x⁴−4x² on ℝ without classifying them.', r'Differentiate termwise::g^{\prime}(x)=4x^3-8x::The polynomial is differentiable everywhere.~Factor before solving::g^{\prime}(x)=4x(x^2-2)::Factoring preserves the possible root x=0.~Set each factor to zero::x=0,\quad x=\pm\sqrt2::These are all real inputs with zero derivative.', '0, √2 and −√2.', 'Substitution in 4x³−8x makes every listed derivative zero; a cubic has no additional roots after this complete factorization.'),
E('trap', 'For 0<x<π, is d/dx ln(sin x)=1/sin x?', r'Identify the domain::::Sine is positive on the given interval, so the real logarithm is defined.~Apply the chain rule::\frac{d}{dx}\ln(\sin x)=\frac{\cos x}{\sin x}::The outer reciprocal must be multiplied by the inner derivative.~Compare at a point::x=\pi/2\Rightarrow f^{\prime}(x)=0::The proposed reciprocal would equal one there, exposing the missing factor.', 'The derivative is cot x, not 1/sin x.', 'Sine has zero slope at π/2, so composing with a differentiable logarithm must also have zero slope at that point.'))

P('0-integration-integrals', 'Integration', r'''
An indefinite integral describes a family of antiderivatives, while a definite integral measures signed accumulation between bounds. The constant of integration accounts for functions with the same derivative on a connected interval. In applications, identify the accumulated quantity and its units: integrating velocity gives displacement, whereas distance requires integrating speed, the absolute value of velocity. Splitting at sign changes is necessary for that second quantity. Antiderivatives must be defined on the relevant interval; a logarithm from a reciprocal integrand needs an absolute value on negative inputs. Verification by differentiation is particularly strong because it tests the entire antiderivative formula rather than only a single numerical value.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Find ∫(3x²−2) dx.', r'Integrate the polynomial term::\int3x^2\,dx=x^3::The power-rule exponent increases and its new coefficient divides the old one.~Integrate the constant::\int-2\,dx=-2x::A linear term differentiates back to the constant.~Add the integration constant::F(x)=x^3-2x+C::All antiderivatives differ by a constant on the real line.', 'x³−2x+C.', 'Differentiating gives 3x²−2 for every real x, independent of C.'),
E('application', 'A particle has velocity v(t)=2t−4 for 0≤t≤3. Find displacement and total distance.', r'Compute signed displacement::\int_0^3(2t-4)\,dt=[t^2-4t]_0^3=-3::Negative velocity contributes negatively.~Locate the reversal::v(t)=0\Rightarrow t=2::Velocity is negative before two and positive after it.~Integrate speed in pieces::-\int_0^2v(t)\,dt+\int_2^3v(t)\,dt=4+1=5::Taking absolute values of the two signed contributions gives traveled distance.', 'Displacement −3; distance 5.', 'The particle moves four units in the negative direction and then one unit back, giving net −3 and total five.'),
E('trap', 'On a negative real interval, what is ∫1/x dx, and why is ln x unsuitable?', r'Check the proposed logarithm::::The real-valued ln x is undefined for negative x.~Use a valid branch::\ln|x|=\ln(-x)\quad(x<0)::This logarithm is defined throughout the negative interval.~Differentiate it::\frac{d}{dx}\ln(-x)=\frac{-1}{-x}=\frac1x::The chain rule verifies the required integrand.', 'ln|x|+C on any connected interval excluding zero.', 'Positive and negative components may have separate constants; no single antiderivative interval crosses the singularity at zero.'))

P('0-integration-integrals', 'Definite integrals', r'''
A definite integral has orientation: reversing bounds negates the accumulated value, even when the integrand is positive. The fundamental theorem evaluates an antiderivative at the upper bound minus its value at the lower bound, provided its assumptions hold. A parameter in a bound remains part of that evaluation and may require a domain condition before solving for it. Singularities inside an interval require separate improper integrals on each side; cancellation of divergent pieces is not an ordinary integral. Symmetric principal values use a different definition and must be named explicitly. Check a computed value against signs, interval width and simple bounds on the integrand before trusting algebra alone.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Evaluate ∫₀²(x+1) dx.', r'Find an antiderivative::F(x)=x^2/2+x::Differentiation returns x+1.~Evaluate the upper bound::F(2)=2+2=4::Use the original endpoint two.~Subtract the lower bound::F(2)-F(0)=4-0=4::Upper minus lower implements the fundamental theorem.', 'Four.', 'The linear function has endpoint heights one and three over width two, giving trapezoid area (1+3)·2/2=4.'),
E('application', 'Find the nonnegative a satisfying ∫₀ᵃ 2x dx=9.', r'Evaluate symbolically::\int_0^a2x\,dx=[x^2]_0^a=a^2::Keep the bound as a parameter.~Solve the resulting equation::a^2=9\Rightarrow a=\pm3::Both algebraic roots must be considered before applying the stated constraint.~Use nonnegativity::a\ge0\Rightarrow a=3::Only the positive root is permitted.', 'a=3.', 'Direct integration to three gives nine; the other root also gives nine algebraically but violates the nonnegative-bound requirement.'),
E('trap', 'Is the usual improper integral ∫₋₁¹ 1/x dx equal to zero by cancellation?', r'Split at the singularity::\int_{-1}^{-\varepsilon}\frac{dx}{x}=\ln\varepsilon::The left integral tends to minus infinity as ε decreases to zero.~Evaluate the right side::\int_{\varepsilon}^{1}\frac{dx}{x}=-\ln\varepsilon::The right integral tends to plus infinity.~Apply the convergence requirement::::Both one-sided improper integrals must converge individually; neither does.', 'The usual improper integral diverges.', 'Symmetric truncation gives principal value zero, but that separate limiting convention cannot replace the divergent one-sided integrals.'))

P('0-integration-substitution-inner-derivative', 'Substitution', r'''
A successful substitution transforms every part of an integral: the inner expression, its differential factor, and definite endpoints when used. It is justified by the chain rule, so an inner derivative appearing in the numerator is a strong clue. If an inverse expression is required, choose the correct branch and ensure it is valid across the interval. A nonmonotone substitution may fold an interval onto itself; splitting into monotone pieces prevents losing orientation or incorrectly selecting a square-root branch. Differentiate the resulting original-variable antiderivative for an independent check. Definite transformed bounds can simplify considerably, but identical new bounds do not license ignoring a substitution that was not valid as written.
''',
E('fundamental', 'For x>−1, evaluate ∫3x²√(1+x³) dx.', r'Choose the inner variable::u=1+x^3,\quad du=3x^2\,dx::The differential matches the entire factor preceding the square root.~Integrate in u::\int u^{1/2}\,du=\frac23u^{3/2}+C::The power rule applies on the positive u-domain.~Return to x::F(x)=\frac23(1+x^3)^{3/2}+C::Undo the substitution in an indefinite integral.', '(2/3)(1+x³)³ᐟ²+C.', 'Differentiating gives (2/3)(3/2)(1+x³)¹ᐟ²·3x², exactly the original integrand.'),
E('application', 'Evaluate ∫₀^{√(ln 2)} 2x exp(x²) dx.', r'Substitute the exponent::u=x^2,\quad du=2x\,dx::The numerator is its derivative.~Transform the bounds::x=0\Rightarrow u=0,\quad x=\sqrt{\ln2}\Rightarrow u=\ln2::The new endpoints simplify the exponential.~Evaluate::\int_0^{\ln2}e^u\,du=e^{\ln2}-1=1::Use the transformed upper and lower limits.', 'One.', 'The original-variable antiderivative exp(x²) has endpoint values two and one, independently giving one.'),
E('trap', 'A student uses u=x² to write ∫₋₁¹ dx=∫₁¹ du/(2√u)=0. Diagnose the error and evaluate correctly.', r'Check the inverse branch::::Writing x=√u describes only x≥0 and omits the negative half of the interval.~Split the interval::\int_{-1}^0dx+\int_0^1dx::On the first piece use x=−√u and on the second use x=√u.~Track both orientations::\int_1^0\frac{-du}{2\sqrt u}+\int_0^1\frac{du}{2\sqrt u}=1+1=2::The branch and bounds together preserve each piece.', 'The integral is two; the single positive inverse branch was invalid over the original interval.', 'Integrating the original constant function directly gives interval length two, confirming the split-substitution result.'))

P('0-integration-techniques', 'Integration by parts', r'''
Integration by parts reallocates differentiation from one factor to another using the product rule in reverse. Choose a factor whose derivative becomes simpler, and ensure the other factor has an accessible antiderivative. For definite integrals, retain the boundary product as well as the remaining integral; neither term is optional. Some choices are valid but unhelpful because they increase algebraic complexity. Repeated integration can produce the original integral again, which is an algebraic equation to solve rather than a failure. Improper endpoints require limits in the boundary term. Check the final formula by differentiating, or verify a numerical value’s sign from the original integrand.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Find ∫x cos x dx.', r'Choose the parts::u=x,\quad dv=\cos x\,dx,\quad du=dx,\quad v=\sin x::Differentiating x lowers its degree.~Apply the formula::\int x\cos x\,dx=x\sin x-\int\sin x\,dx::Both the product and remaining integral are retained.~Integrate the remainder::F(x)=x\sin x+\cos x+C::The negative of the sine antiderivative produces a plus cosine.', 'x sin x+cos x+C.', 'Its derivative is sin x+x cos x−sin x=x cos x.'),
E('application', 'Evaluate the convergent improper integral ∫₀¹ x² ln x dx.', r'Choose parts::u=\ln x,\quad dv=x^2dx,\quad du=dx/x,\quad v=x^3/3::The logarithm simplifies when differentiated.~Check the endpoint product::\lim_{x\to0^+}x^3\ln x=0::The power dominates the logarithmic growth, and the product at one is also zero.~Evaluate the remainder::\int_0^1x^2\ln x\,dx=-\frac13\int_0^1x^2dx=-\frac19::The boundary terms vanish and the remaining polynomial integral is finite.', '−1/9.', 'The integrand is negative on (0,1), agreeing with the answer; differentiating x³ln(x)/3−x³/9 verifies the antiderivative.'),
E('trap', 'After two integrations by parts, ∫eˣsin x dx reappears. Is this failure? Find the antiderivative.', r'Name the recurring integrals::I=\int e^x\sin x\,dx=e^x\sin x-J::Here J is the cosine integral from the first parts step.~Integrate J::J=\int e^x\cos x\,dx=e^x\cos x+I::A second parts step returns I with a sign determined by the derivative of cosine.~Solve the algebraic equation::2I=e^x(\sin x-\cos x)\Rightarrow I=\frac{e^x}{2}(\sin x-\cos x)+C::Returning to I allows it to be collected and solved.', 'eˣ(sin x−cos x)/2+C.', 'Differentiating combines the exponential product terms to 2eˣsin x/2=eˣsin x, verifying the cyclic-parts result.'))

P('0-conditional-bayes-conditioning', 'Conditional probability', r'''
Conditioning changes the reference population to the outcomes where the given event occurred. The numerator counts outcomes satisfying both the target and the condition; the denominator normalizes by the condition, which must have positive probability. In equally likely finite models, this is a ratio of counts within the reduced sample space. Without replacement, later draws depend on earlier ones, so compute joint events through sequential probabilities rather than assuming independence. The direction of conditioning matters because changing the denominator changes the question. Always translate “given” into a specific event before calculating, and verify that the final value lies between zero and one.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Draw one card uniformly from a standard 52-card deck. Given that it is a king, what is the probability it is red?', r'Restrict the sample space::::The condition leaves the four kings, each equally likely.~Count favorable conditioned outcomes::::The heart and diamond kings are red, so two of those four outcomes qualify.~Normalize::P(\text{red}\mid\text{king})=\frac24=\frac12::Use the conditioned four-card population rather than all fifty-two cards.', 'One half.', 'The joint probability is 2/52 and the king probability 4/52; their ratio is again one half.'),
E('application', 'An urn has three red and two blue balls. Draw two without replacement. Given at least one is red, find the probability the first is red.', r'Compute the conditioning probability::P(B)=1-\frac25\frac14=\frac9{10}::The excluded event is two blue draws.~Compute the intersection::P(A\cap B)=P(A)=\frac35::If the first draw is red, at least one red is automatic.~Normalize::P(A\mid B)=\frac{3/5}{9/10}=\frac23::The joint probability is divided by the actual conditioning probability.', 'Two thirds.', 'Among twenty equally likely ordered ball pairs, eighteen contain red and twelve start red; 12/18=2/3.'),
E('trap', 'Among 100 people, 20 belong to A, 50 belong to B, and 10 belong to both. Are P(A|B) and P(B|A) equal?', r'Find the shared numerator::P(A\cap B)=10/100::The intersection event is identical in both directions.~Condition on B::P(A\mid B)=10/50=1/5::The denominator uses the fifty B members.~Condition on A::P(B\mid A)=10/20=1/2::Reversing the question changes the reference population.', 'No; they are 1/5 and 1/2.', 'Multiplying each conditional by its own condition probability gives the same joint value 1/10.'))

P('0-conditional-bayes-bayes', 'Bayes theorem', r'''
Bayes' theorem updates competing explanations after evidence arrives. Multiply each hypothesis’s prior probability by its likelihood for the evidence, then normalize those contributions by their sum. A likelihood is not a posterior: high sensitivity or a high probability of evidence under a hypothesis does not by itself make that hypothesis likely after observing evidence. Repeated observations must use a likelihood for the full observed sequence, with conditional independence stated when products are used. For a finite exhaustive hypothesis partition, total probability provides the denominator. Posterior probabilities should sum to one, and hypotheses with zero prior remain zero unless the model itself is changed.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Choose one of two boxes with equal probability. Box A is one-quarter red; box B is three-quarters red. After drawing red, find the posterior probability of B.', r'Compute joint contributions::P(A\cap R)=\frac12\frac14=\frac18,\quad P(B\cap R)=\frac12\frac34=\frac38::Weight each red likelihood by the box-selection prior.~Find total red probability::P(R)=\frac18+\frac38=\frac12::The two box explanations are exhaustive and disjoint.~Normalize B’s contribution::P(B\mid R)=\frac{3/8}{1/2}=\frac34::The posterior compares B’s contribution with all observed-red outcomes.', 'Three quarters.', 'A has posterior one quarter, so the updated probabilities sum to one and preserve the three-to-one likelihood ratio.'),
E('application', 'Choose a fair coin or a double-headed coin with equal probability. Toss the chosen coin twice, independently conditional on its type, and observe HH. Find the probability it was double-headed.', r'Calculate the sequence likelihoods::P(HH\mid F)=1/4,\quad P(HH\mid D)=1::The two fair tosses multiply, while the double-headed coin always gives heads.~Weight by the priors::P(F\cap HH)=1/8,\quad P(D\cap HH)=1/2::Both coin types initially have probability one half.~Normalize::P(D\mid HH)=\frac{1/2}{1/8+1/2}=\frac45::Use the likelihood of the full two-toss evidence.', 'Four fifths.', 'After one head the posterior is 2/3; updating with the second head gives (2/3)/((2/3)+(1/3)(1/2))=4/5.'),
E('trap', 'A hypothetical condition has prevalence 0.001. A test detects every case but falsely flags 0.01 of noncases. Does a positive result establish the condition with certainty?', r'Compute the true-positive contribution::0.001\cdot1=0.001::Perfect sensitivity does not change the small prior prevalence.~Compute the false-positive contribution::0.999\cdot0.01=0.00999::Noncases are numerous, so this contribution exceeds true positives.~Normalize the posterior::P(\text{case}\mid+) =\frac{0.001}{0.01099}=\frac{100}{1099}::The denominator includes both explanations of a positive result.', 'No; the posterior is 100/1099, about 9.10%.', 'In 100,000 modeled people, there are 100 true positives and 999 false positives, giving the same count ratio.'))

P('0-conditional-bayes-independence-dice-events', 'Independence', r'''
Independence compares a joint probability with a product of marginal probabilities, not with zero overlap. Positive-probability disjoint events are dependent because learning one excludes the other. For three or more events, pairwise independence checks only two-event products; mutual independence additionally checks every larger intersection. A construction from a few fair bits can expose that distinction exactly. Events arising from one experiment may be independent, while events defined from independent inputs can still share a dependent condition. Name the events precisely and compute the intersections using the same outcome model. For zero-probability conditions, the product definition remains meaningful even when a conditional probability ratio is undefined.
''',
E('fundamental', 'On a fair die, let A mean even and B mean divisible by three. Are the events independent?', r'Count the marginals::P(A)=3/6=1/2,\quad P(B)=2/6=1/3::The event sets are {2,4,6} and {3,6}.~Count the intersection::P(A\cap B)=1/6::Only outcome six meets both conditions.~Compare the product::(1/2)(1/3)=1/6::The defining joint-product equality holds.', 'Yes.', 'Given B, the outcomes are {3,6}; one of two is even, so P(A|B)=1/2=P(A).'),
E('application', 'Two independent fair bits are generated. Let A mean first bit zero, B mean second bit zero, and C mean equal bits. Are the three events mutually independent?', r'Find the marginals::P(A)=P(B)=P(C)=1/2::Each event contains two of the four equally likely bit pairs.~Check pairs::P(A\cap B)=P(A\cap C)=P(B\cap C)=1/4::Each pair intersection contains only 00, so pairwise independence holds.~Check the triple::P(A\cap B\cap C)=1/4\ne(1/2)^3=1/8::The triple product fails despite all pairwise checks.', 'Pairwise independent, but not mutually independent.', 'Knowing both A and B determines C with certainty, revealing the remaining dependence directly.'),
E('trap', 'On a fair coin toss, are H and T independent because they are mutually exclusive?', r'Compute their positive marginals::P(H)=P(T)=1/2::Both events have nonzero probability.~Compute the joint::P(H\cap T)=0::One toss cannot be both heads and tails.~Compare the required product::P(H)P(T)=1/4\ne0::Disjointness contradicts independence here.', 'No; they are dependent.', 'P(H|T)=0 differs from P(H)=1/2, confirming that learning tails changes the chance of heads.'))

P('0-random-statistics-random-variable-map', 'Random variables', r'''
A random variable compresses experiment outcomes into numerical information, and its distribution results from that mapping. If the map is many-to-one, combine the probabilities of all preimages of each value. For transformations such as a minimum, a cumulative or survival probability may count those preimages more easily than a direct list. Once a survival function is known for an integer-valued variable, subtract consecutive tails to obtain its mass function. Continuous variables require a density or cumulative function instead of a point-mass table; a density value itself is not the probability of an exact outcome. Keep the underlying experiment and the recorded quantity distinct when interpreting support or events.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Roll a fair die and let X be zero for even outcomes and one for odd outcomes. Find the distribution of X.', r'Define the two preimages::X^{-1}(0)=\{2,4,6\},\quad X^{-1}(1)=\{1,3,5\}::The numerical map groups three outcomes under each value.~Add their probabilities::P(X=0)=P(X=1)=3/6=1/2::Each underlying die face has probability one sixth.~Check the support and total::::Only zero and one are attainable, and their two masses sum to one.', 'X has support {0,1}, each with probability one half.', 'The event X=1 is exactly the odd-die event, providing a direct event-based check.'),
E('application', 'Roll two independent fair dice and let M be their minimum. Find P(M=k) for k=1,…,6.', r'Count the survival event::P(M\ge k)=\left(\frac{7-k}{6}\right)^2::Both dice must land among k,…,6.~Subtract consecutive tails::P(M=k)=\frac{(7-k)^2-(6-k)^2}{36}::Remove cases where both dice exceed k.~Simplify::P(M=k)=\frac{13-2k}{36}::The masses are 11,9,7,5,3,1 divided by thirty-six.', '(13−2k)/36 for k=1,…,6; zero elsewhere.', 'The numerators sum to 36; the tail at k=4 is (3/6)²=1/4, matching (5+3+1)/36.'),
E('trap', 'X is continuous uniform on [0,1], so its density is one. Is P(X=0.5)=1?', r'Distinguish density from mass::::Density must be integrated over an interval to obtain probability.~Measure the singleton::\int_{\{0.5\}}1\,dx=0::A single point has zero width.~Compute an interval for comparison::P(0.4<X<0.6)=0.2::A positive-width interval, rather than one point, carries positive probability.', 'No; the exact-value probability is zero.', 'The CDF is continuous at 0.5, so its jump there, which would represent a point mass, is zero.'))

P('0-random-statistics-variables', 'Mean', r'''
The mean balances weighted values, with observation counts or probabilities supplying the weights. Combining groups therefore requires weighting each group’s mean by its size; a simple average of means works only for equal sizes. Expectation is linear even without independence, but it does not commute with a general nonlinear transformation such as squaring. The mean may be absent from the support because it summarizes the distribution rather than selecting an actual outcome. Check a finite-data mean by verifying that deviations sum to zero, and check a weighted mean by ensuring the weights sum to one. These examples distinguish an arithmetic population summary from a probabilistic expectation while using the same weighted-sum principle.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Find the arithmetic mean of 2,4,9.', r'Compute the total::2+4+9=15::Every observation contributes once.~Count observations::n=3::There are three equally weighted data values.~Divide::\overline x=15/3=5::The arithmetic mean is total divided by count.', 'Five.', 'The deviations are −3,−1,4, whose sum is zero, confirming the balancing property.'),
E('application', 'Thirty students have average score 60 and ten have average score 80. Find the combined average.', r'Recover group totals::30\cdot60=1800,\quad10\cdot80=800::A mean times count gives each group’s total score.~Combine totals and counts::1800+800=2600,\quad30+10=40::Both groups contribute with their actual sizes.~Normalize::2600/40=65::The larger lower-scoring group receives greater weight.', 'Sixty-five.', 'The weighted average (3/4)60+(1/4)80=65 agrees; the unweighted mean seventy would incorrectly treat the groups as equally large.'),
E('trap', 'X equals −1 or 1 with probability one half each. Is E[X²]=(E[X])²?', r'Compute the mean::E[X]=(-1)/2+1/2=0::The opposite values cancel.~Transform before averaging::X^2=1\text{ always}\Rightarrow E[X^2]=1::Squaring removes the sign before expectation.~Compare the operations::1\ne0^2::Nonlinear transformation does not commute with expectation.', 'No; E[X²]=1 while (E[X])²=0.', 'Their difference is variance one, independently confirming that the second moment differs from the squared mean.'))

P('0-random-statistics-central', 'Median', r'''
The median is determined by sorted positions, not by the numerical midpoint of the smallest and largest observations. Frequency tables require cumulative counts to identify the central observations in the expanded dataset. With an even number of numerical observations, use the average of the two central values under the usual convention. Large outliers may leave those central positions unchanged, explaining the median’s resistance to extreme values. Medians of separate groups cannot generally be averaged to recover the combined median because their counts and full ordering matter. A verification should locate how many observations lie on each side of the proposed middle, counting equal values carefully.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Find the median of the unsorted data 9,1,5,3,7.', r'Sort the observations::1,3,5,7,9::The original entry order has no statistical role.~Locate the middle position::(5+1)/2=3::An odd count has one central observation.~Read the middle value::::The third sorted observation is five.', 'Five.', 'Two observations lie below five and two above it, directly confirming the middle position.'),
E('application', 'A frequency table has value 1 occurring twice, value 4 five times, and value 10 once. Find the median.', r'Count the dataset::2+5+1=8::An even total requires two central positions.~Locate cumulative ranges::::Value one occupies positions 1–2, four occupies 3–7, and ten occupies position eight.~Read positions four and five::\operatorname{median}=(4+4)/2=4::Both central observations belong to the value-four block.', 'Four.', 'The expanded list 1,1,4,4,4,4,4,10 has the same two central values.'),
E('trap', 'Group A is {0,0,0} and group B is {100}. Is the combined median the average of the group medians?', r'Compute separate medians::::The medians are zero and one hundred.~Merge and sort::::The combined list is 0,0,0,100, so positions two and three are both zero.~Apply the combined definition::\operatorname{median}=(0+0)/2=0::Averaging the group medians would instead give fifty.', 'No; the combined median is zero.', 'The median depends on the middle observations of the full list, not on treating unequal groups as two equally weighted observations.'))

P('0-random-statistics-central', 'Mode', r'''
The mode identifies a most frequent value and does not involve numerical distance or averaging. Build a frequency table before choosing it; several values can tie for the largest count, giving multiple modes. Under a convention requiring repetition, a dataset of entirely distinct values has no mode, whereas a pure frequency-maximizer convention calls all values tied modes. State that convention in such an edge case rather than silently changing definitions. A large value is not automatically modal, and a mean need not be a mode even when it occurs in the dataset. For categorical observations, the mode remains meaningful where arithmetic mean and median may not be defined.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Find the mode of 3,3,4,4,4,8.', r'Tally frequencies::f(3)=2,\quad f(4)=3,\quad f(8)=1::Count repetitions without regard to magnitude.~Find the largest frequency::\max f=3::Three occurrences exceed the other counts.~Select its value::::Only the value four has this frequency, so the mode is unique.', 'Four.', 'The six tally counts sum to six observations; the largest numerical value eight appears only once.'),
E('application', 'A frequency table has values 2,3,4 with counts 5,3,5. Identify every mode.', r'Compare the counts::5>3::The central value three has fewer occurrences.~Identify the tie::f(2)=f(4)=5::Both outer values attain the maximum frequency.~Report both values::::There is no basis to discard one tied maximizer or average them into a new mode.', 'Modes two and four.', 'The value three is their average but appears only three times, so averaging tied modes would produce a nonmodal value.'),
E('trap', 'Under the convention that a mode must repeat, what is the mode of 1,2,3? Is its mean two automatically a mode?', r'Count each value::f(1)=f(2)=f(3)=1::All observations are distinct.~Apply the stated convention::::No value repeats, so the dataset has no mode.~Distinguish the mean::(1+2+3)/3=2::The arithmetic mean does not satisfy the repetition requirement.', 'No mode under the stated convention; the mean is two.', 'Under a frequency-maximizer convention all three values tie instead. Neither convention makes two a uniquely determined mode.'))

P('0-random-statistics-variables', 'Standard deviation', r'''
Standard deviation measures spread in the original measurement units by taking the nonnegative square root of variance. First calculate the mean, then average squared deviations using probabilities or the appropriate population denominator. Adding a constant shifts every value and the mean equally, leaving deviations unchanged; multiplying by a scalar scales standard deviation by that scalar’s absolute value. A negative scalar cannot produce a negative spread. For observations treated as a sample, the conventional sample variance divides by n−1 rather than n; distinguish that estimator from population variance before computing. The square root of the unbiased variance estimator is not itself generally an unbiased standard-deviation estimator.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Treat data {1,3} as a complete population. Find its standard deviation.', r'Find the mean::\mu=(1+3)/2=2::Both population members carry equal weight.~Average squared deviations::\sigma^2=\frac{(1-2)^2+(3-2)^2}{2}=1::Divide by the full population count.~Take the nonnegative root::\sigma=\sqrt1=1::Standard deviation returns to the data’s original units.', 'One.', 'The second moment is (1²+3²)/2=5; subtracting μ²=4 also gives variance one.'),
E('application', 'A random variable X has standard deviation 2. For Y=−3X+7, find Var(Y) and its standard deviation.', r'Read the original variance::\operatorname{Var}(X)=2^2=4::Variance is the square of standard deviation.~Apply the affine scaling rule::\operatorname{Var}(Y)=(-3)^2\cdot4=36::The translation seven leaves spread unchanged.~Take the square root::\sigma_Y=6::The negative multiplier reverses order but not the sign of spread.', 'Variance 36; standard deviation six.', 'Every centered deviation is multiplied by −3, so its magnitude and the standard deviation are multiplied by three.'),
E('trap', 'Observations are 0,2. Compare population standard deviation with the conventional sample standard deviation.', r'Compute the common deviation sum::\overline x=1,\quad(0-1)^2+(2-1)^2=2::Both conventions center at the same mean.~Use the population denominator::\sigma^2=2/2=1\Rightarrow\sigma=1::The complete two-member population divides by n.~Use the sample denominator::s^2=2/(2-1)=2\Rightarrow s=\sqrt2::The conventional sample variance divides by n−1.', 'Population standard deviation one; sample standard deviation √2.', 'The difference comes entirely from the stated denominator, so the convention must be identified before reporting a number.'))

P('0-binomial-poisson-binomial', 'Binomial distribution', r'''
A binomial count can be written as a sum of success indicators, making its mean and variance transparent. Fixed trial count and identical success probability determine the marginals; independence permits multiplying sequence probabilities and adding indicator variances. Cumulative questions such as “at most one” require adding disjoint masses, while “at least one” often favors a complement. Before applying the model, inspect whether sampling changes the population or success probability. Without replacement from a small population, the count is generally hypergeometric instead. A numerical check can enumerate sequences for a small experiment or calculate the same event through conditional draw probabilities rather than assuming the binomial formula.
''',
E('fundamental', 'Toss a fair coin four times independently. Model the head count X and find its mean and variance.', r'Check the binomial conditions::n=4,\quad p=1/2::The trial count is fixed, each trial is fair, and independence is explicitly given.~Add indicator means::E[X]=4\cdot\frac12=2::Each head indicator has expectation one half.~Add independent variances::\operatorname{Var}(X)=4\cdot\frac12\left(1-\frac12\right)=1::Independence removes covariance terms.', 'X is Binomial(4,1/2), with mean two and variance one.', 'Its masses have numerators 1,4,6,4,1 over sixteen; their weighted mean and squared deviations give the same two and one.'),
E('application', 'Five independent components each fail with probability 0.2. Find the probability that at most one fails.', r'Count zero failures::P(X=0)=0.8^5=0.32768::All five components must work.~Count one failure::P(X=1)=\binom51(0.2)(0.8)^4=0.4096::Choose the failed component, and require the others to work.~Add disjoint masses::P(X\le1)=0.32768+0.4096=0.73728::Exactly zero and exactly one cannot occur together.', '0.73728.', 'The complement probability of at least two failures is 0.26272; the two probabilities sum to one.'),
E('trap', 'Draw two balls without replacement from three red and two blue. Is the red count Binomial(2,3/5)? Test two reds.', r'Compute the actual joint probability::P(RR)=\frac35\frac24=\frac3{10}::After one red draw only two red balls remain among four.~Compute the claimed binomial mass::(3/5)^2=9/25::This incorrectly keeps the second success chance fixed.~Compare the results::3/10\ne9/25::The changing probability and dependence violate the binomial assumptions.', 'No; the without-replacement count is not binomial here.', 'Choosing two red balls among all two-ball subsets gives C(3,2)/C(5,2)=3/10, confirming the actual probability.'))

P('0-binomial-poisson-poisson', 'Poisson distribution', r'''
A Poisson parameter is the expected event count over the specified interval, not the rate by itself. Multiply rate by duration after matching units. Independent Poisson counts add into another Poisson count with summed parameters, which makes combining separate streams straightforward under the independence assumption. The probability mass uses a factorial for an exact count; a tail probability usually sums masses or uses a complement. Mean and variance equal the parameter, while standard deviation is its square root. This equality of count moments does not describe waiting-time moments, which belong to an exponential model. Verify a probability through a recurrence between adjacent masses or through the independent-stream zero-event calculation.
''',
E('fundamental', 'If X is Poisson with parameter two, find P(X=2).', r'Use the exact-count mass::P(X=k)=e^{-2}\frac{2^k}{k!}::The parameter is already an interval mean.~Insert the count::P(X=2)=e^{-2}\frac{4}{2}::Two factorial equals two.~Simplify::P(X=2)=2e^{-2}::The probability is approximately 0.2707.', '2e⁻².', 'Adjacent-mass recurrence P(X=2)=P(X=1)·2/2 agrees with P(X=1)=2e⁻².'),
E('application', 'Independent Poisson streams arrive at two and three events per minute. Find the probability their combined count is positive in thirty seconds.', r'Convert the duration::t=30/60=1/2\text{ minute}::Rates and time must use matching units.~Combine interval means::\lambda=2(1/2)+3(1/2)=5/2::Independence allows the Poisson parameters to add.~Use the zero-count complement::P(X\ge1)=1-e^{-5/2}::Positive count means anything except zero.', '1−e⁻⁵ᐟ², approximately 0.9179.', 'The probability that both streams have zero counts is e⁻¹e⁻³ᐟ²=e⁻⁵ᐟ², independently verifying the complement.'),
E('trap', 'A Poisson count has mean nine. Is its standard deviation also nine?', r'Identify the parameter::\lambda=9::Poisson mean equals its parameter.~Read the variance::\operatorname{Var}(X)=9::It is variance, rather than standard deviation, that equals the mean.~Take the square root::\sigma=\sqrt9=3::Spread in count units uses the nonnegative root.', 'No; its standard deviation is three.', 'Squaring the reported standard deviation returns variance nine; reporting nine would incorrectly imply variance eighty-one.'))

P('0-continuous-distributions-uniform', 'Uniform distribution', r'''
Continuous uniform probability is proportional to length within its support. Its cumulative function rises linearly over that interval, and conditioning on a subinterval produces another uniform distribution on that restricted interval. This makes interval intersections a convenient way to compute conditional probabilities. Equal-length regions have equal probability, but individual real points have zero mass. A discrete uniform distribution on integers uses counts instead of lengths and includes endpoints as actual positive-mass outcomes. Read which model is specified before choosing a numerator. Means and variances provide additional checks on location and support width, while conditional calculations must divide by the length of the allowed region rather than the original whole support.
''',
E('fundamental', 'X is continuous uniform on [0,4]. Find P(X≤1), its mean and variance.', r'Normalize the density::f(x)=1/4\quad(0\le x\le4)::Four units of constant density must integrate to one.~Measure the requested interval::P(X\le1)=1/4::The allowed segment has length one.~Use the support moments::E[X]=(0+4)/2=2,\quad\operatorname{Var}(X)=4^2/12=4/3::The midpoint and squared width determine the moments.', 'Probability 1/4; mean two; variance 4/3.', 'Integrating x/4 gives mean two, and integrating x²/4 gives 16/3; subtracting four yields variance 4/3.'),
E('application', 'X is continuous uniform on [0,10]. Given X>2, find P(X<6).', r'Restrict the support::::The condition leaves the interval (2,10], with length eight.~Intersect the target::::Within the condition, X<6 gives the interval (2,6), with length four.~Normalize the lengths::P(X<6\mid X>2)=4/8=1/2::Uniform density cancels in the conditional ratio.', 'One half.', 'Using original probabilities gives (4/10)/(8/10)=1/2, independently confirming the restriction method.'),
E('trap', 'Compare P(0≤X≤1) for continuous uniform [0,4] and discrete uniform {0,1,2,3,4}.', r'Compute the continuous case::P=\frac{1-0}{4-0}=1/4::Endpoints have zero mass, so interval length is the correct numerator.~Compute the discrete case::P=2/5::The two points zero and one each have mass one fifth.~Compare the models::1/4\ne2/5::Counting included integer endpoints is inappropriate for the continuous model.', 'Continuous: 1/4; discrete: 2/5.', 'The continuous CDF at one is 1/4, while the discrete CDF at one has accumulated two of five point masses.'))

P('0-continuous-distributions-normal', 'Normal distribution', r'''
Normal calculations become simpler after separating location from scale. Symmetry supplies probabilities at the mean directly, while other thresholds require subtracting the mean and dividing by standard deviation. The notation N(μ,σ²) uses variance as its second parameter in these examples. Independent normal sums and differences remain normal, with means combining algebraically and variances adding when the variables are independent. A negative sign changes a variable’s mean contribution but not its variance contribution. Without independence, covariance must be included. Keep exact answers in terms of the standard-normal CDF when no table is provided, and verify whether the requested event is a lower tail, upper tail or interval before using a table entry.
''',
E('fundamental', 'X is normal with mean four and variance sixteen. Find P(X>4) and P(X=4).', r'Use symmetry about the mean::::The left and right halves of the density have equal area.~Split the total probability::P(X>4)=1/2::The continuous distribution assigns no mass to the splitting point.~Evaluate the singleton::P(X=4)=0::A finite density peak is not a point mass.', 'P(X>4)=1/2 and P(X=4)=0.', 'Standardization maps four to zero, and Φ(0)=1/2; continuity of the CDF gives zero probability at exactly that point.'),
E('application', 'Independent normals X and Y have means 10 and 5, variances 4 and 9 respectively. Express P(X>Y).', r'Form the difference::D=X-Y,\quad E[D]=5::The target is D>0.~Combine independent variances::\operatorname{Var}(D)=4+9=13::The negative coefficient of Y squares to one.~Standardize the threshold::P(D>0)=1-\Phi(-5/\sqrt{13})=\Phi(5/\sqrt{13})::Symmetry turns the upper tail into a positive-threshold CDF.', 'Φ(5/√13).', 'The difference is N(5,13), so zero lies below its mean; the resulting probability exceeds one half, as the positive standardized expression indicates.'),
E('trap', 'X is N(0,4), with second parameter explicitly variance. Is P(X≤2)=Φ(1/2)?', r'Convert variance to scale::\sigma=\sqrt4=2::Standardization uses standard deviation, not variance.~Standardize the threshold::z=(2-0)/2=1::Two is one standard deviation above the mean.~Use the proper CDF::P(X\le2)=\Phi(1)::Dividing by four would create the erroneous one-half argument.', 'No; it equals Φ(1), approximately 0.8413.', 'The interval from −2 to 2 is μ±σ and has probability 2Φ(1)−1, consistent with the corrected threshold.'))

P('0-continuous-distributions-exponential', 'Exponential distribution', r'''
An exponential waiting time has constant hazard: conditional chance of surviving an additional interval depends on its length rather than the already elapsed wait. Its rate is inverse time, while mean and median are times and are not equal. Independent exponential clocks can be combined by taking their minimum; survival then requires all clocks to survive, so their rates add. Conditioning on an elapsed event-free period shifts an absolute deadline into a remaining-time interval. Distinguish a probability of waiting longer from the complementary probability of finishing by a deadline. All formulas require a positive rate and nonnegative time, with time units matching the reciprocal units of the rate.
''',
E('fundamental', 'An exponential waiting time has rate 1/2 per minute. Find its median.', r'Write the median condition::P(T\le m)=1/2::A continuous median splits the total probability equally.~Insert the CDF::1-e^{-m/2}=1/2\Rightarrow e^{-m/2}=1/2::The median is a nonnegative time.~Solve logarithmically::m=2\ln2\text{ minutes}::Taking logarithms isolates the time.', '2 ln 2 minutes, about 1.3863.', 'Its survival at this time is exp(−ln2)=1/2. The mean is two minutes, confirming mean and median differ.'),
E('application', 'Independent exponential clocks have rates two and three per minute. Let M be the time until either rings. Find its distribution and mean.', r'Express the joint survival::P(M>t)=P(T_1>t,T_2>t)::Neither clock may ring before t.~Use independence::P(M>t)=e^{-2t}e^{-3t}=e^{-5t}\quad(t\ge0)::The two rates add in the exponent.~Identify the new law::M\text{ is exponential with rate }5,\quad E[M]=1/5::The resulting survival function has the exponential form.', 'Exponential rate five per minute; mean 0.2 minutes.', 'At t=0.2, the individual survival product is e⁻⁰·⁴e⁻⁰·⁶=e⁻¹, matching the new rate.'),
E('trap', 'T is exponential with rate one per minute. Given T>1, is P(T≤4) still 1−e⁻⁴?', r'Identify the conditioned target::1<T\le4::The existing wait is part of the condition, so only this intersection remains.~Normalize its probability::P(T\le4\mid T>1)=\frac{e^{-1}-e^{-4}}{e^{-1}}::Divide the probability of the permitted interval by survival to minute one.~Simplify the remaining-time probability::1-e^{-3}::The deadline is three additional minutes away, not four.', 'No; the conditional probability is 1−e⁻³.', 'Memorylessness identifies the remaining wait with a fresh exponential variable, whose probability of finishing within three minutes is the same result.'))
